#align(center)[#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold")[The rational Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties]]
#align(center)[OpenAI]
#align(center)[September 30, 2026]

== 1 Introduction <sec:introduction>

For a smooth projective complex variety $X$, the rational Hodge conjecture asks whether every rational cohomology class of type $\( p \, p \)$ is a rational linear combination of codimension-$p$ algebraic cycle classes. We prove this assertion for abelian varieties with complex multiplication, in every dimension and codimension.

A complex abelian variety $A$ is of #emph[CM type], or simply #emph[CM], if $"End" \( A \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q)$ contains a commutative semisimple $bb(Q)$-algebra of dimension $2 dim A$. We include the point by convention. Finite products and powers of CM abelian varieties are again CM.

#thm([Theorem 1.1.])[
  #emph[Let $A$ be a complex CM abelian variety. For every integer $p gt.eq 0$, the Betti cycle-class map 
$ "cl"_B : "CH"^p \( A \)_(bb(Q)) arrow.r H^(2 p) \( A \, bb(Q) \) ∩ H^(p \, p) \( A \) $

 is surjective, where $"CH"^p \( A \)_(bb(Q)) = "CH"^p \( A \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q)$ and the intersection is taken in $H^(2 p) \( A \, bb(C) \)$. In particular, the rational Hodge conjecture holds in every codimension on every finite product of complex CM abelian varieties and on every power of such a product.]
] <thm:cm>

The theorem also has consequences beyond the algebraicity of individual CM Hodge classes. The generalized Hodge conjecture predicts algebraic supports of the codimension allowed by the Hodge types of a cohomology substructure. We obtain it for CM abelian varieties by applying the theorem to correspondences from auxiliary CM varieties, following Hazama and Abdulali #cite(label("Hazama2003CM")) #cite(label("Abdulali2005CM")). Two theorems of Milne then give the Tate conjecture for all abelian varieties over finite fields, which asks for algebraic representatives of Galois-invariant $ell$-adic classes, and the Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties in arbitrary characteristic, which asserts positivity of the intersection form on primitive algebraic classes #cite(label("Milne1999Tate")) #cite(label("Milne2002Polarizations")). Section 8 states these conclusions with their coefficient and support conventions and explains the separate implications.

=== 1.1 Hodge classes on CM abelian varieties <hodge-classes-on-cm-abelian-varieties>

The codimension-one case of the Hodge conjecture is the Lefschetz $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ theorem. Hodge formulated the broader problem by asking for an extension of this algebraicity criterion to higher-dimensional cycles #cite(label("Hodge1952")) p. 184. Cup products of divisor classes therefore give algebraic Hodge classes in higher degree. For an abelian variety, $H^(*) \( A \, bb(Q) \) = Lambda^(*) H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$, but this description of its cohomology does not make every Hodge class a linear combination of divisor products. The problem is to construct cycles for the remaining classes.

Weil classes provide an important instance of this difficulty. Suppose an imaginary quadratic field $K$ acts on an abelian variety of dimension $2 n$, with its two embeddings occurring with equal multiplicity in $H^(1 \, 0)$. Then $Lambda_K^(2 n) H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ supplies a two-dimensional rational space of classes of type $\( n \, n \)$. These determinant classes and their generalization to CM fields are discussed in #cite(label("Deligne1982")) Proposition 4.4. Their Hodge type follows from the multiplicity condition; algebraicity requires a geometric construction. Weil classes need not lie in the algebra generated by divisor classes; this occurs already on abelian fourfolds #cite(label("MoonenZarhin1999")) Theorem 0.1. Recent work of Markman proves algebraicity of the Weil classes on polarized abelian sixfolds of Weil type for an imaginary quadratic field, with polarization discriminant $- 1$, and deduces the Hodge conjecture for all abelian fourfolds #cite(label("Markman2025Secant")) Theorem 1.5.1 and Corollary 1.6.1. The sixfold theorem assumes an imaginary quadratic action, without requiring the full CM condition of Theorem 1.

Deligne proved that every Hodge class on an abelian variety is #emph[absolute Hodge]: after transport to a conjugate variety through de Rham cohomology, it remains a Hodge class #cite(label("Deligne1982")) Main Theorem 2.11. This establishes an arithmetic compatibility shared by algebraic cycle classes, but does not by itself produce algebraic cycles. CM varieties play a central role in that argument. Their Hodge groups are tori, and their degree-one cohomology splits, after extending coefficients, into lines indexed by field embeddings. An embedding-line tensor belongs to the scalar extension of the rational Hodge classes exactly when every scalar conjugate has equally many holomorphic and antiholomorphic factors. This is Pohlmann’s balance criterion #cite(label("Pohlmann1968")); see #cite(label("Milne2022")) §1.2(c) and #cite(label("GaoUllmo2026")) Theorem 3.1.

The CM arguments of Deligne and André reduce these Hodge classes to Weil-type classes on auxiliary CM abelian varieties #cite(label("Deligne1982")) #cite(label("Andre1992")). More precisely, the formulation in #cite(label("Milne2022")) Theorem 1, attributed there to André, expresses a CM Hodge class as a sum of pullbacks of split Weil classes. Here #emph[split] refers to the Hermitian polarization form having a totally isotropic subspace of half its dimension. This reduction identifies classes whose algebraicity would suffice; it does not supply their algebraic representatives in all dimensions.

There is also a reduction to relations involving only four labels. Hazama’s arrangement method spans its rational balance kernel by antipodal pairs and four-label square relations #cite(label("Hazama2003CM")) Theorem 7.13; his Corollary 7.14.1 gives the corresponding codimension-two algebraicity reduction. Gao and Ullmo give equivalent quadratic indicator relations and use them, after adjoining auxiliary CM factors, to generate Hodge-induced period relations #cite(label("GaoUllmo2026")) Theorems 7.1–7.2. Their conclusion concerns relations among periods. Our task is to construct algebraic representatives for the corresponding four-factor tensors and to compose those representatives as correspondences. This distinction matters: the reduction uses auxiliary factors and polarization contractions, rather than asserting that low-codimension classes generate the Hodge ring of a fixed power.

=== 1.2 From balanced tensors to surface periods <from-balanced-tensors-to-surface-periods>

Choose a Galois CM field $E$ containing the CM fields in $H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$, and write $G_E = "Gal" \( E \/ bb(Q) \)$ and $c$ for its complex conjugation. An odd sign function $v : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$, satisfying $v \( c tau \) = - v \( tau \)$, specifies a weight-one Hodge structure $U \( v \)$ on the rational vector space $E$. Its embedding line $U \( v \)_lambda$, on which $a in E$ acts as $lambda \( a \)$, has type $\( 1 \, 0 \)$ or $\( 0 \, 1 \)$ according as $v \( lambda \)$ is positive or negative. Section 2 realizes $U \( v \) = H^1 \( B \( v \) \, bb(Q) \)$ for a CM abelian variety $B \( v \)$.

The elementary relation needed in the proof is

$ v_1 + v_2 = v_3 + v_4 . $

It makes every scalar conjugate of the four-factor line

$ U \( v_1 \)_1 ⊗ U \( v_2 \)_1 ⊗ U \( v_3 \)_c ⊗ U \( v_4 \)_c med subset H^4 #h(-0.16667em) (product_(i = 1)^4 B \( v_i \) \, overline(bb(Q))) $

have type $\( 2 \, 2 \)$. We prove that this line lies in the scalar extension of the rational cycle span. Section 2 then assembles arbitrary balanced tensors from these relations: two-row switches connect lists with the same sum, and algebraic polarization pairings contract their intermediate factors. Transfer back to the original abelian variety and descent of coefficients give Theorem 1.

The geometric construction of a four-factor class takes place on a surface. Suppose there are rational Hodge maps $h_i : U \( v_i \) arrow.r H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \)$, for a smooth projective surface $S$, and vectors $alpha_i$ on the four selected embedding lines such that

$ integral_S h_1 \( alpha_1 \) smile h_2 \( alpha_2 \) smile h_3 \( alpha_3 \) smile h_4 \( alpha_4 \) eq.not 0 . $

Degree-one Hodge maps are induced, up to rational multiples, by morphisms $S arrow.r B \( v_i \)$. The image of $S$ in their product therefore gives an algebraic cycle whose pairing with the desired line is nonzero. Section 3 shows how four algebraic divisor kernels convert this functional into a vector on that exact line. The remaining problem is thus to find four degree-one classes with both a nonzero surface period and the prescribed rational CM sources.

=== 1.3 Theta forms and their CM sources <theta-forms-and-their-cm-sources>

Section 4 constructs the period on a compact arithmetic quotient of the complex two-ball. Its unitary group has signature $\( 2 \, 1 \)$ at one distinguished embedding and is definite at all the other real places. After the elementary normalization in Section 2, all four lines $U \( v_i \)_1$ are holomorphic at that embedding. To each type the construction assigns a family of holomorphic theta one-forms. Their values span a cotangent fiber, so two such families supply a wedge that is a nonzero holomorphic two-form.

To compare the two sides of the four-factor relation, we realize each pair of types by an orthogonal decomposition of the same Hermitian plane. The balance relation says that the two decompositions have the same local sign multiplicities. Weak approximation produces a rational second decomposition whose compact-place data are as close as desired to those of the first. At the distinguished embedding, the total period data depend only on the plane and remain unchanged.

A rank-two theta tensor packages the wedges into one total two-form. We keep its finite theta input and the compact quotient fixed while varying the compact-place data. The total form varies continuously, so its pairing with the initial nonzero form stays close to the nonzero self-pairing. At the two rational endpoints it is a finite sum of wedges of the required one-forms. Factoring these endpoints and passing to a common finite cover therefore gives a nonzero mixed period with two holomorphic and two antiholomorphic factors.

The construction uses Weil’s rational theta covariance and the unitary splittings of the Weil representation #cite(label("Weil1964")) #cite(label("Kudla1994")) #cite(label("BoradeEtAl2025ToriPeriods")). Kudla and Millson developed differential-form theta kernels in this unitary setting #cite(label("KudlaMillson1990DifferentialTheta")) Introduction and Theorem 3. The projection turning the rank-two tensor into wedges has a close predecessor in the pure-cocycle product calculation of Bergeron, Millson, and Mœglin #cite(label("BMM2016")) Lemmas 5.7 and 5.11. We derive the required tensor identity in coordinates and use the fixed-input variation to obtain the mixed period.

These are initially complex one-form classes. The surface criterion also requires that they come from specified rational CM Hodge structures. Sections 5 and 6 establish this arithmetic input. First we realize the ball quotients in a projective moduli space and construct algebraic Hecke endomorphisms of the product of its components’ Albanese varieties. Counting ordinary isogeny neighbors gives a polynomial relation between these operators and Frobenius. We then compute the Hecke parameters of the theta forms. The Frobenius relation and the degree-one Weil weight force slope zero or one on the relevant Hecke summands, with the choice determined by the prescribed CM signs.

The Hecke projectors preserve the Hodge filtration, so weak admissibility turns these extreme slopes into pure Hodge types. Applying the calculation to every scalar conjugate identifies the complete CM type of each summand. Scalar descent then expresses every relevant theta class as a complex linear combination of images of the selected CM line under rational Hodge maps. Section 7 puts the finite families on one projective surface, expands the nonzero period, and applies the surface criterion. This completes the four-factor construction and hence the proof of the main theorem.

== 2 CM tensors and four-factor relations <sec:tensors>

This section reduces Theorem 1 to the algebraicity of a specified family of tensors in the fourth cohomology of products of four CM abelian varieties. The reduction changes the CM types of individual factors while preserving their total Hodge type; its intermediate factors may therefore be absent from the original variety. We first record how to work with individual embedding lines and recover rationality after extending coefficients. We then construct the auxiliary CM varieties and assemble arbitrary balanced tensors from four-factor relations by algebraic correspondences.

=== 2.1 Scalar coefficients and degree-one maps <scalar-coefficients-and-degree-one-maps>

For a smooth projective complex variety $X$, write

$ "Hdg"^(2 p) \( X \) = H^(2 p) \( X \, bb(Q) \) ∩ H^(p \, p) \( X \) \, #h(2em) cal(Z)^(2 p) \( X \) = "im" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "CH"^p \( X \)_(bb(Q)) arrow.r H^(2 p) \( X \, bb(Q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

Cohomology is Betti cohomology unless another theory is specified. We fix $overline(bb(Q)) subset bb(C)$, and write $T_k = T ⊗_(bb(Q)) k$ for a rational vector space $T$. A class with coefficients in $overline(bb(Q))$ or $bb(C)$ is called #emph[algebraic] if it belongs to the corresponding scalar extension of $cal(Z)^(2 p) \( X \)$. Scalar conjugation therefore preserves algebraicity on the same complex variety: it fixes each rational cycle class and changes only its coefficient. We use the compatibility of cycle classes with exterior products, cup products, pullback between smooth varieties, and proper pushforward.

Our Tate convention is $V \( r \)^(a \, b) = V^(a + r \, b + r)$, so $V \( r \)$ has weight $"wt" \( V \) - 2 r$.

#thm([Lemma 2.1.])[
  (Scalar descent).  #emph[Let $T$ be a finite-dimensional rational vector space and $P subset T_(bb(C))$ a complex-linear subspace. If $t in T_(overline(bb(Q)))$ and $sigma t in P$ for every $sigma in "Gal" \( overline(bb(Q)) \/ bb(Q) \)$, then 
$ t in \( T ∩ P \) ⊗_(bb(Q)) overline(bb(Q)) . $

 The same assertion applies to the subspace of maps of any fixed Hodge type in a Hom space.]
] <lem:trace-descent>

#proof[
  Choose a finite Galois extension $K \/ bb(Q)$ containing the coefficients of $t$. For $a in K$, the vector
  $ T_a = "Tr"_(K \/ bb(Q)) \( a t \) = sum_(sigma in "Gal" \( K \/ bb(Q) \)) sigma \( a \) sigma \( t \) $
  is rational. Each automorphism of $K$ extends to $overline(bb(Q))$, so every summand belongs to $P$; hence $T_a in T ∩ P$. If $b_1 \, dots.h \, b_r$ is a rational basis of $K$ and $b_1^(*) \, dots.h \, b_r^(*)$ is its trace-dual basis, write $t = sum_j t_j b_j$ with $t_j in T$. Then $T_(b_j^(*)) = t_j$, which proves the assertion. The condition that a complex-linear map have a fixed Hodge type is complex-linear, so the same argument applies in a Hom space. ◻
]

We will also use

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.1)")
 $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] cal(Z)^(2 p) \( X \) ⊗_(bb(Q)) overline(bb(Q)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] ∩ H^(2 p) \( X \, bb(Q) \) = cal(Z)^(2 p) \( X \) . $ <eq:cycle-descent>]

Indeed, extend a rational basis of $cal(Z)^(2 p) \( X \)$ to one of $H^(2 p) \( X \, bb(Q) \)$ and compare coefficients.

#thm([Lemma 2.2.])[
  (Realization in degree one).  #emph[For complex abelian varieties $A$ and $B$, pullback induces an isomorphism 
$ "Hom" \( A \, B \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q) med tilde.eq med "Hom"_(upright("Hdg")) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H^1 \( B \, bb(Q) \) \, H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

 If $S$ is a smooth connected projective complex variety and $h : H^1 \( B \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \)$ is a rational Hodge map, then there are an integer $m gt.eq 1$ and a morphism $f : S arrow.r B$ such that $f^(*) = m h$ on $H^1 \( B \, bb(Q) \)$.]
] <lem:weight-one-maps>

#proof[
  Dualizing a rational Hodge map gives a rational map on homology commuting with the complex structures. Multiplication by a positive integer makes it carry the integral lattice of $A$ into that of $B$. It consequently induces a holomorphic homomorphism of complex tori $A^(upright("an")) arrow.r B^(upright("an"))$, which is algebraic. Conversely, an algebraic homomorphism induces the indicated Hodge pullback. This proves the first assertion; equivalently it is the dual of the rational form of Riemann’s theorem #cite(label("MilneShimura2017")) Proposition 6.5, Theorems 6.7–6.8, and Corollary 6.9.
  The Albanese map of $S$ induces an isomorphism $H^1 \( "Alb" S \, bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \)$. Apply the first assertion to the map with target $H^1 \( "Alb" S \, bb(Q) \)$, clear a denominator, and compose the resulting homomorphism $"Alb" S arrow.r B$ with an Albanese map. ◻
]

The Lefschetz $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ theorem says that $"Hdg"^2 \( X \) = cal(Z)^2 \( X \)$. In conjunction with Lemma 2, it has the following useful consequence: if $t in H^2 \( X \, overline(bb(Q)) \)$ is of type $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ in every scalar conjugate, then $t$ is algebraic. This observation will supply all two-factor tensors used below.

=== 2.2 Rank-one CM structures <rank-one-cm-structures>

For a polarizable rational Hodge structure $H$ of weight one, let $J \( H \)$ be the smallest rational algebraic subgroup of $"GL" \( H \)$ containing the Hodge circle. The circle acts by $z$ on $H^(1 \, 0)$ and by $macron(z)$ on $H^(0 \, 1)$, for $\| z \| = 1$. It preserves an alternating polarization. Its connectedness shows that it lies in the rational identity component of $J \( H \)$; minimality therefore makes $J \( H \)$ connected.

#thm([Lemma 2.3.])[
  #emph[For a complex abelian variety $A$, the following conditions are equivalent:]
  + #emph[$J \( H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \) \)$ is a torus.]
  + #emph[$H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ is a direct sum of rational Hodge structures, each of rank one over a CM field acting by Hodge endomorphisms.]
  + #emph[$"End" \( A \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q)$ contains a commutative semisimple algebra of rational dimension $2 dim A$.]
] <lem:toric-cm>

#proof[
  The zero-dimensional case is immediate. Put $H = H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$, and first assume that $J \( H \)$ is a torus. Its associative envelope $E_0 subset "End"_(bb(Q)) \( H \)$ is a commutative semisimple algebra: after splitting the torus, its elements act diagonally by scalars on the weight spaces, so the envelope is a subalgebra of a product of fields and is reduced. This description descends to $bb(Q)$.
  Let $*$ be the polarization adjoint. It preserves $E_0$, since it sends every symplectic torus element to its inverse. Every element of $E_0$ commutes with the complex structure $I$ supplied by the Hodge circle. Consequently $*$ is also the adjoint for the positive definite real form obtained by inserting $I$ in the polarization. In particular,
  $ "Tr"_(H_(bb(R))) \( a^(*) a \) > 0 #h(2em) \( 0 eq.not a in E_0 ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \) . $
  The involution cannot exchange two primitive factors of the real finite étale algebra $E_0 ⊗ bb(R)$: for the idempotent $e$ of one factor, this would give $e^(*) e = 0$. On a complex factor it cannot be the identity, since for its idempotent $e$ one would have $\( i e \)^(*) \( i e \) = - e$, of negative trace. Thus it acts as complex conjugation on each complex factor. There are no real factors. On the nonzero module of such a factor the circle would act by real scalars, and a continuous homomorphism from the circle to $bb(R)^times$ is trivial; this contradicts the weight-one characters.
  It follows that each rational field factor $K$ of $E_0$ is CM. Indeed, it has only complex archimedean factors, and its involution satisfies $tau \( a^(*) \) = overline(tau \( a \))$ at every embedding. Its fixed field is therefore totally real and has index two. On the corresponding $K$-module the circle acts by $K ⊗ bb(R)$-scalars. Splitting that module into $K$-lines thus gives rational Hodge substructures. This proves (ii), without discarding multiplicities.
  The separate scalar actions on the field lines in (ii) form a commutative semisimple Hodge-endomorphism algebra of dimension $dim_(bb(Q)) H$. Lemma 3 realizes it in $"End" \( A \) ⊗ bb(Q)$, proving (iii). Conversely, write an algebra as in (iii) as $product_j K_j$. Its faithful action on $H$ has a positive rank $m_j$ over each $K_j$, and
  $ dim_(bb(Q)) H = sum_j m_j \[ K_j : bb(Q) \] = sum_j \[ K_j : bb(Q) \] . $
  Hence every $m_j = 1$. The centralizer of this action in $"GL" \( H \)$ is the torus $product_j "Res"_(K_j \/ bb(Q)) upright(bold(G))_m$. It contains the Hodge circle because the algebra acts by Hodge endomorphisms, proving (i). ◻
]

These equivalences give the rank-one description of the CM varieties defined in the introduction. A finite product of CM abelian varieties is CM: the circle on the direct sum of their degree-one cohomologies lies in the product of their tori, and its connected rational envelope is a subtorus.

Choose a Galois CM field $E subset overline(bb(Q))$, and put

$ F = E^(+) \, #h(2em) G_E = "Gal" \( E \/ bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) s = \[ F : bb(Q) \] . $

Its complex conjugation $c in G_E$ is central. One such $E$ can contain any finite collection of CM fields and all their conjugates. To see this, take the compositum of all those conjugates. For a CM field $K$, every embedding $rho : K arrow.r bb(C)$ satisfies $rho c_K = c_(bb(C)) rho$. These identities show that complex conjugation on the Galois compositum commutes with its Galois group. Every conjugate of an element fixed by it is real, so the fixed field is totally real. The compositum is therefore a Galois CM field.

An #emph[odd sign function] on $G_E$ is a function $v : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$ satisfying $v \( c tau \) = - v \( tau \)$. Give the rational vector space $E$ a weight-one Hodge structure $U \( v \)$ by declaring its embedding line

$ U \( v \)_lambda = { u in U \( v \)_(overline(bb(Q))) : a u = lambda \( a \) u upright(" for every ") a in E } #h(2em) \( lambda in G_E \) $

to have type $\( 1 \, 0 \)$ if $v \( lambda \) = 1$, and type $\( 0 \, 1 \)$ otherwise. Oddness makes these declarations compatible with the real structure.

This Hodge structure is polarizable. Choose $0 eq.not delta in E$ with $c \( delta \) = - delta$. Multiplying $delta$ by an element of $F^times$, weak approximation gives an element $xi in E^times$ such that $c \( xi \) = - xi$ and

$ "sgn" #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] "Im" tau \( xi \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = - v \( tau \) $

at one representative $tau$ of each conjugate pair. The form

$ psi_v \( x \, y \) = "Tr"_(E \/ bb(Q)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] xi x c \( y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $

is alternating and nondegenerate. It pairs only conjugate embedding lines. On a vector $u$ in the sum of the $\( 1 \, 0 \)$ lines, $i psi_v \( u \, macron(u) \)$ is a sum of positive multiples of the squared absolute values of its embedding coordinates; hence it is positive for $u eq.not 0$. This is the weight-one polarization condition. After scaling, the form is integral on a lattice. The Riemann criterion, applied to the dual homological Hodge structure, gives a CM abelian variety $B \( v \)$ of dimension $s$ with

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.2)")
 $ U \( v \) = H^1 \( B \( v \) \, bb(Q) \) . $ <eq:cm-types>]

This construction permits every odd sign function, including imprimitive CM types; it does not require a principal polarization. The polarization and Riemann-criterion argument also applies to any CM field, without a Galois hypothesis, and any CM type, that is, any choice of one embedding from each conjugate pair. The Galois field $E$ provides a common indexing set for the types used below.

We will need the exact action of scalar conjugation on labels. If $sigma in "Gal" \( overline(bb(Q)) \/ bb(Q) \)$ restricts to $g in G_E$ and $u in U \( v \)_lambda$, rationality of the $E$-action gives

$ a thin sigma \( u \) = sigma \( a u \) = sigma \( lambda \( a \) \) sigma \( u \) = \( g lambda \) \( a \) sigma \( u \) . $

Consequently

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.3)")
 $ sigma U \( v \)_lambda = U \( v \)_(g lambda) . $ <eq:cm-label-conjugation>]

In particular the complete type signs of $U \( v \)_1$ and $U \( v \)_c$ after $g$ are $v \( g \)$ and $- v \( g \)$, respectively. Here and below the commutation of $c$ with $g$ takes place in $G_E$; no centrality assertion is made in $"Gal" \( overline(bb(Q)) \/ bb(Q) \)$.

The same convention applies to a line in a rank-one module over a CM subfield of $E$. Choose $lambda in G_E$ extending the embedding that labels the line. Its #emph[complete type function] takes the value $+ 1$ at $g in G_E$ if the line labeled by the restriction of $g lambda$ has type $\( 1 \, 0 \)$, and $- 1$ if it has type $\( 0 \, 1 \)$. It is odd. Since the embedding-line decomposition is defined over $E$, this is the type of the scalar-conjugate line for every automorphism of $overline(bb(Q))$ restricting to $g$.

#thm([Lemma 2.4.])[
  (Transfer of an embedding line).  #emph[Let $H$ and $H'$ be direct sums of rank-one weight-one Hodge modules over CM fields contained in $E$. Choose an embedding line $L subset H_(overline(bb(Q)))$ and an embedding line $M subset H'_(overline(bb(Q)))$ with the same complete type function. Extend any $overline(bb(Q))$-linear map $T : L arrow.r M$ by zero on all other source embedding lines. Then 
$ T in "Hom"_(upright("Hdg")) \( H \, H' \) ⊗_(bb(Q)) overline(bb(Q)) . $

 When $H$ and $H'$ are degree-one cohomologies of abelian varieties, this map is therefore an algebraic-coefficient linear combination of pullbacks of abelian morphisms, up to rational denominators.]
] <lem:cm-line-transfer>

#proof[
  Scalar conjugation transports the entire source and target decompositions. For each $sigma$, the only possibly nonzero component of $sigma T$ maps $sigma L$ to $sigma M$, whose types agree by hypothesis. All other components are zero. Thus $sigma T$ is a Hodge map on the whole source. Apply Lemma 2 in $"Hom"_(bb(Q)) \( H \, H' \)$, followed, when applicable, by Lemma 3. ◻
]

The map $T$ in this lemma need not be invariant under a stabilizer of either line. This matters when a field is a proper subfield of $E$, or when a rational field module occurs more than once. The proof treats every scalar conjugate of the zero-extended map and retains the repeated rational summands separately.

=== 2.3 Reduction to four factors <reduction-to-four-factors>

The four-factor assertion that remains to be proved is the following.

#thm([Theorem 2.5.])[
  (Four-factor switch).  #emph[Let $E$ be a Galois CM field and let $v_1 \, v_2 \, v_3 \, v_4 : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$ be odd sign functions satisfying $v_1 + v_2 = v_3 + v_4$. Under the Künneth inclusion, the following line is algebraic: 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.4)")
 $ U \( v_1 \)_1 ⊗ U \( v_2 \)_1 ⊗ U \( v_3 \)_c ⊗ U \( v_4 \)_c med subset med H^4 #h(-0.16667em) (product_(i = 1)^4 B \( v_i \) \, overline(bb(Q))) $ <eq:switch-line>]

]
] <thm:switch>

This line is already of Hodge type $\( 2 \, 2 \)$ in every scalar conjugate. Indeed, after an automorphism restricting to $g in G_E$, its four type signs have sum

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.5)")
 $ v_1 \( g \) + v_2 \( g \) - v_3 \( g \) - v_4 \( g \) = 0 . $ <eq:switch-conjugate-balance>]

The theorem asks for algebraicity, which does not follow from this balance alone. We leave its proof to the subsequent construction; the present section proves that it is sufficient.

Hazama’s coordinate-arrangement calculation rationally spans the kernel of its Hodge matrix by antipodal pairs and elementary four-label relations #cite(label("Hazama2003CM")) Theorem 7.13. His Corollary 7.14.1 states a corresponding codimension-two algebraicity reduction. We prove the particular tensor reduction needed here directly, including the transfers to auxiliary abelian factors and the algebraic contractions that assemble the relations.

#thm([Proposition 2.6.])[
  #emph[If Theorem 6 holds for every Galois CM field and every quadruple of odd sign functions satisfying its relation, then Theorem 1 holds.]
] <prop:switch-reduction>

#proof[
  Let $A$ be a CM abelian variety and let $h in "Hdg"^(2 p) \( A \)$. The cases $p = 0$ and $p = 1$ follow from the unit class and the Lefschetz $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ theorem, respectively. For higher codimension, we first replace a Hodge monomial by a tensor on auxiliary CM factors. We then connect its two lists of types by four-factor switches and compose the resulting algebraic tensors.
  By Lemma 4, choose a rational decomposition of $H = H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ into rank-one CM-field modules, retaining separate copies of repeated summands. Choose a Galois CM field $E$ containing all the fields and their conjugates. Over $E$, $H$ is the direct sum of its embedding lines. Since cup product gives $H^(*) \( A \, bb(Q) \) = Lambda^(*) H$, the exterior monomials in these lines give a direct-sum decomposition of $H^(*) \( A \, overline(bb(Q)) \)$ into one-dimensional lines #cite(label("MilneShimura2017")) Proposition 6.4. Every such line is contained in one Hodge bidegree.
  Scalar conjugation permutes the monomial lines, with only the possible sign from reordering exterior factors. Because $h$ is rational, a nonzero component on one monomial line gives a nonzero component on every line in its scalar-Galois orbit. All these lines must have type $\( p \, p \)$. The decomposition is defined over $E$, so the components of $h$ have algebraic Betti coefficients.
  Fix a supported monomial and list the complete type functions of its $2 p$ factors as $z_1 \, dots.h \, z_(2 p)$. Each $z_i$ is odd. In the conjugate indexed by $g$, the number of $\( 1 \, 0 \)$ factors minus the number of $\( 0 \, 1 \)$ factors is $sum_i z_i \( g \)$. Its balance is therefore exactly
  $ sum_(i = 1)^(2 p) z_i = 0 . $
  For $1 lt.eq i lt.eq p$, set $x_i = z_i$ and $y_i = - z_(p + i)$. Then $sum_i x_i = sum_i y_i$. The first $p$ factors have the complete types of $U \( x_i \)_1$, and the last $p$ have those of $U \( y_i \)_c$. For any ordered list $a = \( a_1 \, dots.h \, a_p \)$ of odd sign functions, put $A_a = product_(i = 1)^p B \( a_i \)$; for two such lists put
  $ cal(L)_(a \, b) = ⨂_(i = 1)^p U \( a_i \)_1 med ⊗ med ⨂_(i = 1)^p U \( b_i \)_c subset H^(2 p) \( A_a times A_b \, overline(bb(Q)) \) . $
  Repeated factors in these products are kept as separate slots. Lemma 5 reduces the chosen monomial to the algebraicity of $cal(L)_(x \, y)$. We return to $A$ after composing the transition tensors.
  Regard each list as a matrix with $p$ rows and one column for each representative of $G_E \/ chevron.l c chevron.r$. At any column, equality of the two sums is equality of the numbers of plus signs. Among the rows where the current $x$-entry differs from the desired $y$-entry, there are equally many mismatches of the forms $\( + 1 \, - 1 \)$ and $\( - 1 \, + 1 \)$. Pair these two sets of rows and swap the two current entries in every pair, making the opposite swap at the conjugate coordinate. This corrects the column, leaves the other columns unchanged, and preserves oddness of every row. Successively correcting the columns gives a finite sequence
  $ x = x^(\( 0 \)) \, med x^(\( 1 \)) \, med dots.h \, med x^(\( N \)) = y $
  in which consecutive lists differ by one such two-row swap.
  At a single swap the old rows $a \, b$ and new rows $a' \, b'$ satisfy $a + b = a' + b'$ at every coordinate. Theorem 6 supplies the pure algebraic tensor
  $ U \( a \)_1 ⊗ U \( b \)_1 ⊗ U \( a' \)_c ⊗ U \( b' \)_c . $
  Every unchanged row is joined to its new copy by $U \( v \)_1 ⊗ U \( v \)_c$, an all-conjugate $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ line and hence an algebraic two-factor tensor. Pull these classes to their distinct old and new slots and multiply. This gives a nonzero algebraic generator of $cal(L)_(x^(\( j \)) \, x^(\( j + 1 \)))$. If the original lists already agree, the two-factor tensors alone generate $cal(L)_(x \, x)$. It remains to compose consecutive transition tensors without losing their algebraicity or nonvanishing.
  For three ordered lists $a \, b \, e$, suppose that $cal(L)_(a \, b)$ and $cal(L)_(b \, e)$ have pure algebraic generators with algebraic Betti coefficients. Write them as
  $ t_(a b) & = alpha_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ alpha_p ⊗ beta_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ beta_p \,\
t_(b e) & = gamma_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ gamma_p ⊗ delta_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ delta_p \, $
  where $beta_i in U \( b_i \)_c$ and $gamma_i in U \( b_i \)_1$. Identify the middle copies and choose an ample divisor class $ell_i$ on each $B \( b_i \)$. With $r_(a b) \, r_(b e) \, r_(a e)$ denoting the projections from $A_a times A_b times A_e$, and with the $ell_i$ also denoting their pullbacks, cup product and pushforward give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.6)")
   $ \( r_(a e) \)_(*) (r_(a b)^(*) t_(a b) smile r_(b e)^(*) t_(b e) smile product_(i = 1)^p ell_i^(thin s - 1)) & = \( - 1 \)^(p \( p - 1 \) \/ 2) (product_(i = 1)^p q_i) t_(a e) \,\
q_i & = integral_(B \( b_i \)) beta_i and gamma_i and ell_i^(thin s - 1) \, $ <eq:transition-composition>]
  where $t_(a e) = alpha_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ alpha_p ⊗ delta_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ delta_p$. The sign is obtained by regrouping $beta_1 dots.h.c beta_p gamma_1 dots.h.c gamma_p$ into $beta_1 gamma_1 dots.h.c beta_p gamma_p$ in the indicated slot order.
  Each $q_i$ is nonzero. Complex conjugation exchanges the two middle embedding lines, so $gamma_i$ is a nonzero scalar multiple of $macron(beta)_i$. Hodge–Riemann positivity for the ample class makes the pairing with $macron(beta)_i$ nonzero, whether $beta_i$ is holomorphic or antiholomorphic. This requires no compatibility between $ell_i$ and $E$. Moreover $q_i in overline(bb(Q))^times$, since its vectors have algebraic Betti coefficients and $ell_i$ is rational. The codimension on the left of Equation (2.6) is
  $ p + p + p \( s - 1 \) - p s = p . $
  Thus it is an algebraic class of the required degree, and division by the nonzero algebraic coefficient shows that the endpoint line is algebraic. Iterating along the sequence of lists proves algebraicity of $cal(L)_(x \, y)$.
  Finally choose the line transfers in the direction from the auxiliary $U$’s to $H^1 \( A \, bb(Q) \)$. Each is an algebraic-coefficient linear combination of rational pullbacks along maps from $A$ to the corresponding $B \( v \)$. Apply their exterior product on $A^(2 p)$ and pull back along the diagonal $A arrow.r A^(2 p)$. The result is the cup product of the selected original factors. They occupy distinct embedding or multiplicity lines in the original exterior monomial, so this cup product is nonzero. We have proved that every supported monomial line is algebraic. Summing the components of $h$ and using Equation (2.1) proves the rational assertion.
  Finite products are covered because they are themselves CM, as observed after Lemma 4. ◻
]

The proof uses tensors on auxiliary products and contracts their intermediate slots before returning to $A$. It does not assert generation of the Hodge ring on a fixed power by cup products of classes in codimensions one and two.

#thm([Lemma 2.7.])[
  (Choice of the distinguished embedding).  #emph[It suffices to prove Theorem 6 when $s gt.eq 2$ and $v_i \( 1 \) = 1$ for all four functions.]
] <lem:switch-normalization>

#proof[
  If $v_1$ and $v_2$ are nowhere simultaneously positive, oddness also rules out simultaneous negativity. Hence $v_2 = - v_1$. The relation gives $v_4 = - v_3$, and the first two and the last two slots of Equation (2.4) are separately all-conjugate $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ pairs.
  Otherwise choose $g in G_E$ at which $v_1 \( g \) = v_2 \( g \) = 1$. The relation makes all four signs positive there. Choose a scalar automorphism extending $g$. It sends the labels in Equation (2.4) to $g \, g \, g c \, g c$. The complete type function of the line labeled $g$ is the right translate
  $ v_i^g \( tau \) = v_i \( tau g \) . $
  It is odd, satisfies $v_i^g \( 1 \) = 1$, and the four translated functions satisfy the same relation. Centrality of $c$ in $G_E$ makes the complete type of the $g c$-line equal to $- v_i^g$. The transfers of Lemma 5 therefore carry the normalized switch for the $v_i^g$’s to the lines labeled $g \, g \, g c \, g c$ on the original factors. Applying the inverse scalar automorphism gives Equation (2.4).
  When $s = 1$, there is only one odd sign function positive at the identity, so the normalized tensor is a product of ordinary pairs. ◻
]

For the remaining construction we work in the normalized case, so all four lines $U \( v_i \)_1$ are holomorphic. The next section isolates a surface-period condition sufficient to prove the switch.

== 3 A surface criterion for the switch <sec:surface>

To prove Theorem 6, we will construct four degree-one classes on one surface with a nonzero cup product. The criterion below explains why this is sufficient. The four maps give an algebraic surface class that tests the switch line nontrivially; four divisor classes then convert that test into a vector on the line itself.

#thm([Proposition 3.1.])[
  (Surface criterion).  #emph[Let $E$ be a Galois CM field with conjugation $c$, and let $v_1 \, v_2 \, v_3 \, v_4 : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$ be odd sign functions satisfying $v_1 + v_2 = v_3 + v_4$. Put $lambda = \( 1 \, 1 \, c \, c \)$. Suppose that $S$ is a smooth connected projective complex surface and that there are rational Hodge maps 
$ h_i : U \( v_i \) arrow.r H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) 1 lt.eq i lt.eq 4 \, $

 and nonzero vectors $alpha_i in U \( v_i \)_(lambda_i) subset U \( v_i \)_(overline(bb(Q)))$ such that 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ integral_S h_1 \( alpha_1 \) smile h_2 \( alpha_2 \) smile h_3 \( alpha_3 \) smile h_4 \( alpha_4 \) eq.not 0 . $ <eq:surface-period>]

 Here each $h_i$ is extended to $overline(bb(Q))$-coefficients. Then the line in Equation (2.4) is algebraic.]
] <prop:surface-switch>

#proof[
  By Lemma 3, there are positive integers $m_i$ and morphisms $f_i : S arrow.r B \( v_i \)$ with $f_i^(*) = m_i h_i$. Therefore
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
   $ k := integral_S f_1^(*) alpha_1 smile f_2^(*) alpha_2 smile f_3^(*) alpha_3 smile f_4^(*) alpha_4 in overline(bb(Q))^times . $ <eq:surface-morphism-period>]
  Nonvanishing follows from Equation (3.1), since the new value is multiplied by $product_i m_i$. The value is algebraic because the pullbacks, cup product, and evaluation on $\[ S \]$ are rational operations on Betti cohomology and the $alpha_i$ have algebraic Betti coefficients.
  Put $A = product_i B \( v_i \)$, $n = dim A = 4 s$, and $f = \( f_i \) : S arrow.r A$. Let
  $ z = f_(*) \[ S \] in "CH"^(n - 2) \( A \) \, #h(2em) Z = "cl" \( z \) in cal(Z)^(2 n - 4) \( A \) . $
  For $t_alpha = alpha_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ alpha_4$ in the four-$H^1$ Künneth summand of $H^4 \( A \, overline(bb(Q)) \)$, the projection formula gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
   $ integral_A Z smile t_alpha = k . $ <eq:surface-functional>]
  Thus $Z$ gives a nonzero functional on the switch line; it is not itself a vector on that line. In particular $f \( S \)$ cannot have dimension less than two, since then the proper pushforward of the two-cycle $\[ S \]$ and the displayed pairing would be zero.
  For each $i$, take a copy $B \( v_i \)'$ with its transported $E$-action, and choose a nonzero vector $beta_i in U \( v_i \)'_(c lambda_i)$ with algebraic Betti coefficients. Under the Künneth inclusion define the pure class
  $ D_i = alpha_i ⊗ beta_i in H^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] B \( v_i \) times B \( v_i \)' \, overline(bb(Q)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  If $sigma in "Gal" \( overline(bb(Q)) \/ bb(Q) \)$ restricts to $g in G_E$, then Equation (2.3) puts its two factors on the lines labeled
  $ g lambda_i \, #h(2em) g c lambda_i = c g lambda_i . $
  These have opposite Hodge types. This uses centrality of $c$ in $G_E$ only, and remains valid when the scalar coordinates of $alpha_i$ and $beta_i$ lie outside $E$. Every scalar conjugate of $D_i$ therefore has type $\( 1 \, 1 \)$. Scalar descent and the Lefschetz $\( 1 \, 1 \)$ theorem give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
   $ D_i in cal(Z)^2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] B \( v_i \) times B \( v_i \)' #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] ⊗_(bb(Q)) overline(bb(Q)) . $ <eq:surface-divisor-kernels>]
  The entire cohomology class $D_i$ is algebraic here. It was defined in the pure $H^1 ⊗ H^1$ summand before applying descent, so there is no need to project an arbitrary divisor class onto that summand.
  Set $A' = product_i B \( v_i \)'$. Let $p : A times A' arrow.r A$ and $p' : A times A' arrow.r A'$ be the projections, and let $q_i : A times A' arrow.r B \( v_i \) times B \( v_i \)'$ select the $i$-th old and new slots. Write
  $ K = product_(i = 1)^4 q_i^(*) D_i \, #h(2em) t_beta = beta_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ beta_4 . $
  Order the old factors first and the new factors second in the Künneth identification for $A times A'$. Moving the new degree-one factor of $D_i$ past the later old degree-one factors makes $3 + 2 + 1 = 6$ sign changes. Hence
  $ K = \( - 1 \)^6 thin t_alpha ⊗ t_beta = t_alpha ⊗ t_beta . $
  In particular $K$ has degree four in the old factors and degree four in the new factors. Integration over $A$ consequently gives the exact formula
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.5)")
   $ p'_(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p^(*) Z smile K #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = (integral_A Z smile t_alpha) t_beta = k thin t_beta . $ <eq:surface-to-tensor>]
  Any other Künneth component of $Z$ contributes zero to this integration. The codimension of the left side is $\( n - 2 \) + 4 - n = 2$, equivalently its cohomological degree is $\( 2 n - 4 \) + 8 - 2 n = 4$.
  By Equation (3.4), the left side of Equation (3.5) is algebraic with $overline(bb(Q))$-coefficients. Since $k in overline(bb(Q))^times$, division by $k$ proves algebraicity of the line generated by $t_beta$, whose labels are $\( c \, c \, 1 \, 1 \)$. Ordinary scalar complex conjugation fixes the rational cycle span on the same $A'$ and changes these labels to $\( 1 \, 1 \, c \, c \)$. Identifying the copied factors with the original ones gives the asserted line on $A$. ◻
]

The theta construction produces complex classes, and the arithmetic argument will place them in spans of rational Hodge images. The following consequence applies to those classes.

#thm([Corollary 3.2.])[
  (Surface criterion for complex spans).  #emph[Let $E$, $v_1 \, dots.h \, v_4$, and $lambda = \( 1 \, 1 \, c \, c \)$ be as in Proposition 9, and let $S$ be a smooth connected projective complex surface. Suppose that, for each $1 lt.eq i lt.eq 4$, a class $eta_i in H^1 \( S \, bb(C) \)$ belongs to 
$ "span"_(bb(C)) { h \( U \( v_i \)_(lambda_i) \) : h in "Hom"_(upright("Hdg")) \( U \( v_i \) \, H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \) \) } $

 and that 
$ integral_S eta_1 smile eta_2 smile eta_3 smile eta_4 eq.not 0 . $

 Then the line in Equation (2.4) is algebraic.]
] <cor:surface-span>

#proof[
  Fix a nonzero algebraic Betti generator of each selected line. Each $eta_i$ is a finite complex linear combination of its images under rational Hodge maps. Expanding the nonzero cup product selects a nonzero term of the form in Equation (3.1). Thus Proposition 9 applies. This argument uses only finite sums; all four maps have the same target $H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \)$. ◻
]

== 4 Theta differentials and a nonzero mixed period <sec:theta>

The surface criterion of Proposition 9 asks for a nonzero period made from four one-forms on one surface. We construct the analytic part of that input using theta series attached to auxiliary Hermitian spaces of dimensions one and two, called multiplicity spaces. For a multiplicity line, a component of the first Taylor tensor is a holomorphic one-form. Varying the finite theta input makes its values span a cotangent fiber, so two such constructions give a nonzero wedge. For their product input, the second Taylor tensor for the orthogonal sum of the two lines has a double-alternating projection that is a nonzero multiple of that wedge. This total two-form allows us to change the two multiplicity lines while keeping their sum and its finite theta input fixed.

For the two sign pairs in the normalized switch, we will obtain total two-forms $xi \, xi'$ on one fixed compact ball quotient $X$. On a common principal cover $pi : X' arrow.r X$, they have finite expansions into holomorphic one-forms:

$ pi^(*) xi = sum_r a_r Theta_1 \( phi.alt_(1 \, r) \) and Theta_2 \( phi.alt_(2 \, r) \) \, #h(2em) pi^(*) xi' = sum_s b_s Theta_3 \( phi.alt_(3 \, s) \) and Theta_4 \( phi.alt_(4 \, s) \) $

such that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ integral_(X') pi^(*) xi and overline(pi^(*) xi') eq.not 0 . $ <eq:theta-mixed-period-goal>]

The comparison of $xi'$ with $xi$ keeps the rational symplectic space, the rational special-unitary group, the total finite theta input, and the compact quotient fixed. Only afterward will we factor the rational endpoints and pass to $X'$, on which the individual one-forms in both sums descend. At this stage they give complex cohomology classes. Section 5 will provide projective principal levels, and Section 6 will identify the rational CM source maps.

=== 4.1 The ball and the sign data <subsec:theta-ball>

Let $E subset overline(bb(Q))$ be a Galois CM field, put $F = E^(+)$, and assume $s = \[ F : bb(Q) \] gt.eq 2$. Write $G_E = "Gal" \( E \/ bb(Q) \)$ and let $c in G_E$ be complex conjugation. Fix a set $Sigma subset G_E$ of representatives modulo $c$, including the distinguished embedding $mu = 1$. The construction for a single multiplicity line takes an odd sign function $v : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$ with $v \( 1 \) = 1$. We index its signs by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ d \( tau \) = v \( tau^(- 1) \) \, #h(2em) d \( c tau \) = - d \( tau \) \, #h(2em) d \( mu \) = 1 . $ <eq:theta-sign-inversion>]

Thus $d \( g^(- 1) \) = v \( g \)$, the indexing needed by the arithmetic calculation. For the normalized switch, fix four such functions with $d_1 + d_2 = d_3 + d_4$. We first construct the one-form for an arbitrary one of them, and then compare the pairs $\( d_1 \, d_2 \)$ and $\( d_3 \, d_4 \)$. All Hermitian forms in this section are linear in their first argument.

Choose an $E \/ F$-Hermitian space $V$ of $E$-dimension three, of signature $\( 2 \, 1 \)$ at $mu$ and positive definite at every other real place of $F$. For example, take a diagonal form with two totally positive coefficients and a third coefficient negative at $mu$ and positive elsewhere; weak approximation in $F$ supplies the last coefficient. Put

$ G = "SU" \( V \) \, #h(2em) D = { thin L subset V_mu : dim_(bb(C)) L = 1 \, med h_V \|_L < 0 thin } . $

The group $G$ is defined over $F$, and $D$ is the complex two-ball. We also write $L$ for the tautological negative line bundle over $D$, and set $Q = underline(V_mu) \/ L$. The Grassmannian tangent calculation gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ T_D = "Hom" \( L \, Q \) \, #h(2em) Omega_D^1 = L ⊗ Q^(*) . $ <eq:theta-cotangent>]

Fix an $cal(O)_E$-lattice $Lambda subset V$; all principal congruence levels below are matrix levels for this lattice. Their quotients of $D$ are compact. To see the relevant anisotropy, an isotropic vector defined over $E$ would still be isotropic at a definite real completion, which is impossible. Equivalently, $G$ has no nontrivial $F$-split torus: a nonzero weight space of such a torus in $V$ would be isotropic, since invariance of $h_V$ forces $h_V \( x \, x \) = 0$ for a vector of nontrivial split weight. Thus $G$ is $F$-anisotropic. The arithmetic lattice and compactness theorems therefore make its arithmetic subgroups cocompact lattices in $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$ #cite(label("BorelHarishChandra1962")); see also the formulation in #cite(label("MilneShimura2017")) Theorem 3.3 and Proposition 3.5. Here

$ G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \) tilde.eq "SU" \( 2 \, 1 \) times product_(tau eq.not mu) "SU" \( 3 \) . $

Projection past the compact factors preserves discreteness and cocompactness. Indeed, a sequence in an arithmetic subgroup whose active projections approach the identity has, after taking a subsequence, convergent compact components; differences would then approach the identity in the full discrete subgroup. A sufficiently deep principal matrix level is torsion-free. Its action on $D$ is free, because a discrete subgroup has finite intersection with a point stabilizer. Hence its quotient is a smooth connected compact Kähler surface. Later we will impose a principal level for which the reference moduli construction also proves projectivity.

=== 4.2 Positive Lagrangians and differential bundles <subsec:theta-lagrangians>

Fix $delta in E^times$ with $c \( delta \) = - delta$. For a nondegenerate $E \/ F$-Hermitian space $P$ of $E$-dimension $n in { 1 \, 2 }$, let

$ bb(V)_P = "Res"_(E \/ F) \( V ⊗_E P \) . $

It is an $F$-space of dimension $6 n$. The product of the two Hermitian forms is a well-defined Hermitian form on $V ⊗_E P$, and we use the $F$-valued form

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
 $ psi_P \( v ⊗ p \, v' ⊗ p' \) = "Tr"_(E \/ F) #h(-0.16667em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] delta thin h_V \( v \, v' \) h_P \( p \, p' \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:theta-symplectic-form>]

Interchanging the arguments conjugates the Hermitian product and changes the sign of $delta$, so $psi_P$ is alternating. It is nondegenerate: the tensor-product Hermitian form is nondegenerate, and the trace pairing on $E \/ F$ is nondegenerate. More explicitly, if the Hermitian pairing of $x eq.not 0$ with some $y$ is $b eq.not 0$, then its pairing with $e y$, for $e in E$, is $c \( e \) b$; trace duality supplies $e$ for which $"Tr"_(E \/ F) \( delta c \( e \) b \) eq.not 0$. Thus $\( bb(V)_P \, psi_P \)$ is a symplectic space over $F$. Its underlying rational vector space carries the alternating form $"Tr"_(F \/ bb(Q)) psi_P$; this is the restriction of scalars used in complexifications over $bb(Q)$ below. Symplectic coordinates and transformations will still be taken over $F$.

First take $P = W_i$ to be a Hermitian line associated with a sign function $d_i$ as in (4.2). Complexifying the trace pairing identifies the $c tau$ summand complex-linearly with the dual of the $tau$ summand. Define a subbundle $cal(F)_i subset bb(V)_P ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(C)$ over $D$ at the active pair of embeddings by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
 $ \( cal(F)_i \)_mu = L ⊗ \( W_i \)_mu \, #h(2em) \( cal(F)_i \)_(c mu) = Q^(*) ⊗ \( W_i \)_mu^(*) . $ <eq:theta-active-lagrangian>]

Here $Q^(*) = "ann" \( L \) subset V_mu^(*)$, embedded in the conjugate summand by the Hermitian pairing. It is the holomorphic annihilator bundle, not an antiholomorphically varying orthogonal complement in $V_mu$. At $tau in Sigma \\ { mu }$, take the full $c tau$ summand when $d_i \( tau \) = 1$, and the full $tau$ summand when $d_i \( tau \) = - 1$; take zero on the other summand. For a rational orthogonal sum $P = W_1 perp W_2$, take the direct sum of these prescriptions and write $cal(F)_P = cal(F)_1 xor cal(F)_2$.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  #emph[The Hermitian forms on the lines $W_i$ can be chosen over $E \/ F$ so that $cal(F)_P$ is a holomorphic positive Lagrangian for $psi_P$, equivariantly for $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$. Its determinant and an equivariant square root have the form 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
 $ det cal(F)_P tilde.eq L^(2 n) ⊗ C_P \, #h(2em) \( det cal(F)_P \)^(1 \/ 2) tilde.eq L^n ⊗ C_P^(1 \/ 2) \, $ <eq:theta-halfdet>]

 where $C_P$ is a constant complex line on which $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$ acts trivially.]
] <lem:theta-positive-lagrangian>

#proof[
  Write $tau \( delta \) = i t_tau$, where $t_tau in bb(R)^times$. At the real place represented by $tau in Sigma$, write $psi_(P \, tau)$ for the complex bilinear extension of the real-place form. Our positivity convention is
  $ psi_(P \, tau) \( u \, overline(u) \) \/ i > 0 #h(2em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] 0 eq.not u in \( cal(F)_P \)_tau xor \( cal(F)_P \)_(c tau) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $
  On the $tau$ summand its sign is the sign of $t_tau h_V h_P$; on the $c tau$ summand the sign is the opposite. At $mu$, choose the coefficient of each line form to have sign $- "sgn" \( t_mu \)$. The negative line $L$ on the $mu$ side and the dual of the positive plane on the $c mu$ side then both have positive sign. At a definite place $tau eq.not mu$, choose the line coefficient to have sign
  $ - d_i \( tau \) "sgn" \( t_tau \) . $
  The chosen full summand has positive sign in either case. These finitely many strict sign conditions on an element of $F$ are simultaneously realizable by weak approximation.
  At the active pair the two subspaces are annihilators under the complexified pairing; at every other pair one side is full and the other zero. Thus $cal(F)_P$ is isotropic of half the total dimension. The active annihilator description proves holomorphy, and the compact-place summands are constant over $D$. Equivariance follows from the action on $L$ and its annihilator at $mu$, and from preservation of the full embedding summands at the compact places.
  Finally
  $ det Q^(*) = L ⊗ det \( V_mu \)^(*) . $
  For general $P$ the active determinant is therefore
  $ det \( L ⊗ P_mu \) ⊗ det \( Q^(*) ⊗ P_mu^(*) \) tilde.eq L^(2 n) ⊗ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] det \( V_mu \)^(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^(⊗ n) ⊗ det \( P_mu \)^(*) . $
  All factors other than $L^(2 n)$, including the determinants at the compact places, are constant lines. Their special-unitary actions are trivial, because they involve powers of determinants of $V_tau$ or $V_tau^(*)$, while $G$ acts trivially on $P$. This proves the first isomorphism in (4.6); choosing a square root of the constant line gives the second. ◻
]

We choose nonzero complex vectors in these constant lines when identifying tensors with differential forms. Such choices only rescale those forms by nonzero constants. No rational trivialization is asserted. Also, the constant lines are trivial for $G = "SU" \( V \)$; they need not be trivial under the larger unitary group.

The determinant calculation gives the differential targets for the theta tensors. For a multiplicity line $W_i$, the relevant target is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.7)")
 $ \( det cal(F)_i \)^(1 \/ 2) ⊗ \( cal(F)_i \)_(c mu) & tilde.eq L ⊗ Q^(*) ⊗ \( upright("constant line") \)\
 & tilde.eq Omega_D^1 ⊗ \( upright("constant line") \) . $ <eq:theta-one-target>]

For $W = W_1 perp W_2$, define the double-alternating map

$ "alt"_2 : "Sym"^2 \( Q^(*) ⊗ W_mu^(*) \) arrow.r Lambda^2 Q^(*) ⊗ Lambda^2 W_mu^(*) $

by

$ "alt"_2 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( a ⊗ u \) \( b ⊗ v \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = \( a and b \) ⊗ \( u and v \) . $

Here the product on the left is multiplication in the symmetric algebra. Exchanging its two factors changes both exterior signs, so the map is well-defined. Including the half-determinant line gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.8)")
 $ \( det cal(F)_W \)^(1 \/ 2) ⊗ Lambda^2 Q^(*) ⊗ Lambda^2 W_mu^(*) tilde.eq K_D ⊗ \( upright("constant line") \) \, $ <eq:theta-top-target>]

because $K_D = det Omega_D^1 = L^2 ⊗ Lambda^2 Q^(*)$. The first theta tensor will give the one-form in (4.7); the double-alternating part of the second will give the two-form in (4.8).

The reason to combine the two lines is geometric. At the active pair, their total positive Lagrangian has the pieces

$ L ⊗ W_mu \, #h(2em) Q^(*) ⊗ W_mu^(*) . $

These depend only on $W_mu$, independently of its decomposition into lines. At a compact place, let $W_tau^(+)$ and $W_tau^(-)$ be the sums of the multiplicity lines with respective signs $+ 1$ and $- 1$ under $d_i$. The total positive pieces there are

$ V_tau ⊗ W_tau^(-) \, #h(2em) V_tau^(*) ⊗ \( W_tau^(+) \)^(*) \; $

the second dual is embedded as $"ann" \( W_tau^(-) \) subset W_tau^(*)$. The relation $d_1 + d_2 = d_3 + d_4$ says that the two sign pairs have the same multiset at each place. Thus the original local lines can be relabeled with signs $d_3 \, d_4$ without changing these total pieces. Weak approximation will produce a rational decomposition with the new signs and nearby compact-place data, while leaving the active total Lagrangian unchanged. We will apply this approximation in Subsection 4.7 to one fixed theta input. To compare the resulting forms on a compact quotient, we first establish their covariance and congruence descent.

=== 4.3 Rational theta covariance <subsec:theta-covariance>

Choose symplectic coordinates over $F$ on $bb(V)_P$, written $\( p \, q \) in F^a xor F^a$, where $a = 3 n$, with

$ psi_P \( \( p \, q \) \, \( p' \, q' \) \) = p^t q' - q^t p' . $

The Schrödinger position coordinates are $q$. A positive Lagrangian at a real place has a unique graph frame

$ e_Z = binom(Z, I) \, #h(2em) Z = Z^t \, #h(2em) "Im" Z > 0 . $

Indeed, it cannot meet the $p$-axis nontrivially, because $psi_P \( u \, macron(u) \)$ vanishes there. Projection to the $q$-axis is therefore an isomorphism. Isotropy says $Z^t = Z$, and, for a graph vector,

$ psi_(P \, tau) \( e_Z u \, overline(e_Z u) \) \/ i = 2 u^t \( "Im" Z \) macron(u) \, $

which proves the last condition. The positive Lagrangians above thus give a period map $D arrow.r product_(tau in Sigma) frak(H)_a$.

Let $phi.alt in cal(S) \( bb(A)_(F \, f)^a \)$ be a finite-adelic Schwartz–Bruhat function. For $Z = \( Z_tau \)_tau in product_tau frak(H)_a$ and $z = \( z_tau \)_tau in xor.big_tau bb(C)^a$, put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.9)")
 $ theta.alt_phi.alt \( Z \, z \) = sum_(x in F^a) phi.alt \( x \) exp #h(-0.16667em) (pi i sum_(tau in Sigma) x_tau^t Z_tau x_tau + 2 pi i sum_(tau in Sigma) x_tau^t z_tau) . $ <eq:theta-series>]

The rational transformation law for this series includes an exponential quadratic in $z$, which mixes its ordinary Taylor coefficients. The following completion cancels that factor and gives coefficients with a tensor transformation law. Define

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.10)")
 $ hat(theta.alt)_phi.alt \( Z \, z \) & = exp #h(-0.16667em) (pi / 2 sum_(tau in Sigma) z_tau^t \( "Im" Z_tau \)^(- 1) z_tau) theta.alt_phi.alt \( Z \, z \) \,\
hat(theta.alt)_phi.alt \( Z \, z \) & = sum_(k gt.eq 0) chevron.l T_(k \, phi.alt) \( Z \) \, z^(⊗ k) chevron.r . $ <eq:theta-completion>]

Thus $T_(k \, phi.alt)$ includes the factor $1 \/ k !$ in the $k$-th derivative. We use multiplication in the symmetric algebra for these homogeneous coefficients.

The series and every fixed order of its derivatives converge uniformly on compact subsets of the Siegel and $z$ domains. Indeed, the rational points in the support of $phi.alt$ lie in finitely many translates of a full lattice in $\( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)^a$. On a compact set the imaginary parts have a common positive lower bound, and the summands, after a fixed number of differentiations, are bounded by a polynomial in $parallel x parallel$ times

$ C exp \( - c parallel x parallel^2 + C' parallel x parallel \) . $

The lattice sum of this bound converges. The completion and its derivatives obey the same bounds on compact sets. The same conclusion holds after any fixed number of derivatives in smooth parameters on a compact subset of a smooth parameter domain: there the period map and all derivatives of it needed for that order are bounded, and its imaginary parts have a common positive lower bound. Compactness of the image of a period map alone, without bounds on its parameter derivatives, would not imply this last assertion.

We next specify the rational Weil-representation input, including the cover being used. The circle extension is obtained from the metaplectic double cover by extending its central ${ 1 \, - 1 }$ to the group of unit complex scalars. Take the global additive character of $bb(A)_F \/ F$ whose infinite component is $t mapsto exp \( 2 pi i "Tr"_(F \/ bb(Q)) t \)$. Weil’s rational section in the adelic circle extension leaves the summation functional

$ lambda \( Phi \) = sum_(x in F^a) Phi \( x \) $

invariant. In characteristic zero his pseudosymplectic group is the symplectic group, and his adelic twofold subgroup contains this rational section #cite(label("Weil1964")) Ch. III, nos. 31 and 41, Eq. (39) and Theorem 6; Ch. IV, no. 45, pp. 200–201. Consequently, for a rational symplectic transformation we may choose an infinite double-cover lift and its paired finite lift whose adelic product is the rational lift. A change of the infinite sign requires the same change of the finite sign. These paired lifts give rational covariance; they are not an assertion that the symplectic double cover splits on an arbitrary adelic unitary group.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  #emph[Let $g in "Sp" \( bb(V)_P \) \( F \)$. At each real place write 
$ g = mat(delim: "(", A, B; C, D_0) \, #h(2em) J = C Z + D_0 \, #h(2em) g Z = \( A Z + B \) J^(- 1) . $

 Choose an infinite metaplectic lift with factor $j \( g \, Z \)$ satisfying $j \( g \, Z \)^2 = product_(tau in Sigma) det J_tau$, and let $tilde(g)_f$ be its paired finite lift. Then 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.11)")
 $ theta.alt_(omega_f \( tilde(g)_f \) phi.alt) \( g Z \, J^(- t) z \) = j \( g \, Z \) exp #h(-0.16667em) (pi i sum_(tau in Sigma) z_tau^t J_tau^(- 1) C_tau z_tau) theta.alt_phi.alt \( Z \, z \) . $ <eq:theta-jacobi-law>]

 The completed coefficients satisfy 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.12)")
 $ T_(k \, omega_f \( tilde(g)_f \) phi.alt) \( g Z \) = j \( g \, Z \) "Sym"^k \( J \) thin T_(k \, phi.alt) \( Z \) . $ <eq:theta-taylor-law>]

 They are thus tensors valued in 
$ \( det cal(F)_P \)^(1 \/ 2) ⊗ "Sym"^k cal(F)_P . $

 In particular the positive Lagrangian, not its dual, occurs in the symmetric factor. The tensor $T_(1 \, phi.alt)$ is holomorphic.]
] <lem:theta-tensor-law>

#proof[
  We first check the infinite formula, keeping track of its signs. At one real place write
  $ f_(Z \, z) \( x \) = exp \( pi i x^t Z x + 2 pi i x^t z \) \, #h(2em) K \( g \, Z \) = J^(- 1) C . $
  The matrix $K \( g \, Z \)$ is symmetric; this follows from $J C^t = C J^t$, a symplectic identity. The formula to prove is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.13)")
   $ omega_oo \( tilde(g) \) f_(Z \, z) = j \( g \, Z \)^(- 1) exp \( - pi i z^t K \( g \, Z \) z \) f_(g Z \, J^(- t) z) . $ <eq:theta-gaussian-law>]
  For symmetric $B$, the upper unipotent $n \( B \) = mat(delim: "(", I, B; 0, I)$ acts by multiplication by $exp \( pi i x^t B x \)$, which gives (4.13) with $j = 1$. For $m \( A \) = "diag" \( A \, A^(- t) \)$, the chosen lift acts by
  $ omega_oo \( tilde(m) \( A \) \) b \( x \) = j \( m \( A \) \)^(- 1) b \( A^t x \) \, #h(2em) j \( m \( A \) \)^2 = \( det A \)^(- 1) . $
  For $det A > 0$ the positive choice has $j \( m \( A \) \)^(- 1) = \| det A \|^(1 \/ 2)$. For negative determinant the metaplectic phase is retained in $j$. Since $m \( A \) Z = A Z A^t$ and $J^(- t) z = A z$, this is the required formula.
  For $w = mat(delim: "(", 0, - I; I, 0)$, choose the lift
  $ b mapsto e^(- pi i a \/ 4) integral_(bb(R)^a) b \( y \) e^(- 2 pi i x^t y) thin d y . $
  The Gaussian integral is
  $ integral_(bb(R)^a) f_(Z \, z) \( y \) e^(- 2 pi i x^t y) thin d y = det \( - i Z \)^(- 1 \/ 2) exp #h(-0.16667em) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] - pi i \( x - z \)^t Z^(- 1) \( x - z \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  where the square root of $det \( - i Z \)$ equals $1$ at $Z = i I$. Taking
  $ j \( w \, Z \) = e^(pi i a \/ 4) det \( - i Z \)^(1 \/ 2) $
  gives $j \( w \, Z \)^2 = det Z$ and proves (4.13) for $w$.
  These generators give the formula for the whole real symplectic group. To check that the displayed exponent also composes correctly, let $J_2 = J \( g_2 \, Z \)$ and $J_1 = J \( g_1 \, g_2 Z \)$. The symplectic identities give
  $ J \( g_1 g_2 \, Z \) = J_1 J_2 \, #h(2em) K \( g_1 g_2 \, Z \) = K \( g_2 \, Z \) + J_2^(- 1) K \( g_1 \, g_2 Z \) J_2^(- t) . $
  For the second identity, use $A_2 - \( g_2 Z \) C_2 = J_2^(- t)$ to write $C_12 = C_1 J_2^(- t) + J_1 C_2$, and multiply by $\( J_1 J_2 \)^(- 1)$. Together with the metaplectic group law for $j$, this is exactly the composition law in (4.13). Multiplying the formula over the real places gives its infinite adelic version.
  Apply rational invariance of $lambda$ to $phi.alt ⊗ product_tau f_(Z_tau \, z_tau)$. By (4.13), the transformed sum is
  $ j \( g \, Z \)^(- 1) exp #h(-0.16667em) (- pi i sum_tau z_tau^t J_tau^(- 1) C_tau z_tau) theta.alt_(omega_f \( tilde(g)_f \) phi.alt) \( g Z \, J^(- t) z \) . $
  Equating this with the original sum gives (4.11). In particular the finite action on the left is $omega_f \( tilde(g)_f \)$, with no inverse. If one instead keeps the finite input on the left fixed, the input on the right is acted on by $omega_f \( tilde(g)_f \)^(- 1)$.
  For the completion, put $Y = "Im" Z$ and $Y' = "Im" \( g Z \)$ at one place. From $Y' = macron(J)^(- t) Y J^(- 1)$, and then $macron(J)^t = J^t - 2 i Y C^t$, we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.14)")
   $ J^(- 1) \( Y' \)^(- 1) J^(- t) = Y^(- 1) - 2 i J^(- 1) C . $ <eq:theta-completion-identity>]
  Here $C^t J^(- t) = J^(- 1) C$ by symmetry. Thus the completion factor at $\( g Z \, J^(- t) z \)$ differs from that at $\( Z \, z \)$ by $exp \( - pi i z^t J^(- 1) C z \)$. This cancels the exponential in (4.11), so
  $ hat(theta.alt)_(omega_f \( tilde(g)_f \) phi.alt) \( g Z \, J^(- t) z \) = j \( g \, Z \) hat(theta.alt)_phi.alt \( Z \, z \) . $
  Comparison of homogeneous terms yields (4.12). Finally,
  $ g e_Z = e_(g Z) J . $
  Hence $J$ is the transformation matrix for vectors in the positive Lagrangian; this proves the asserted tensor target and its orientation. The completion has no linear term in $z$, so $T_(1 \, phi.alt)$ is the ordinary holomorphic theta gradient. For comparison, the first-order law agrees with the classical theta-gradient formula in #cite(label("MollerZagier2016")) §9.1. ◻
]

For a multiplicity line $W_i$, the $c mu$-projection of $T_(1 \, phi.alt)$ therefore lies in the cotangent target (4.7). Rational covariance alone does not supply a congruence subgroup fixing a given finite input. Subsection 4.4 obtains such stabilizers from an honest smooth finite-adelic action.

#prodef([Remark 4.3.])[
  We will use the lemma to change rational Schrödinger coordinates. Suppose a rational symplectic matrix $S$ takes adapted coordinate vectors to the reference coordinate vectors. Then $Z_(upright("ref")) = S Z_(upright("ad"))$, and the input in the adapted coordinates is
  $ phi.alt_(upright("ad")) = omega_f \( tilde(S)_f \)^(- 1) phi.alt_(upright("ref")) . $
  Equation (4.12) compares the resulting tensors. Changing the sign of the paired lift changes both the infinite factor and the finite input sign, so their comparison is unchanged. For coordinates adapted to a rational symplectic direct sum, the period matrix is block diagonal and the completed theta series of a product input is the product of the two completed series.
] <rem:theta-adapted-coordinates>

=== 4.4 Unitary splittings and congruence descent <subsec:theta-splittings>

The preceding lemma uses paired lifts of rational symplectic elements. An honest action on finite inputs, and therefore a congruence stabilizer for each input, requires a different statement. We use the unitary splitting theorem in the following precise form. For a quadratic extension $E \/ F$, a Hermitian space $H$ of dimension $m$, a skew-Hermitian space $K$ of dimension $n$, and a global additive character, choose unitary Hecke characters $chi_H \, chi_K$ of $E$ with

$ chi_H \|_(bb(A)_F^times \/ F^times) = epsilon.alt_(E \/ F)^(thin m) \, #h(2em) chi_K \|_(bb(A)_F^times \/ F^times) = epsilon.alt_(E \/ F)^(thin n) . $

The corresponding unitary actions lift to the #emph[circle-scalar extension] of the adelic symplectic Weil representation. The global lift is assembled from local lifts and agrees with the canonical rational symplectic section on rational unitary points. Its finite part acts smoothly on finite Schwartz functions. Changing the splitting characters twists the unitary actions by characters composed with their determinants. Here $epsilon.alt_(E \/ F)$ is the quadratic idele class character.

The local splitting is due to Kudla #cite(label("Kudla1994")). We use the circle-extension formulation and its local and adelic compatibility in #cite(label("BoradeEtAl2025ToriPeriods")) §§3.1–3.1.1 and 4.1–4.1.1. In the present CM setting, adelic automorphy and the determinant-twist rule are also recorded in #cite(label("BMM2016")) §7.2 and Remark 7.3. Throughout this invocation the extension is the circle pushout: we do not assert that the double cover splits on every unitary group. We will compare this circle lift with an actual double lift only after restriction to $G = "SU" \( V \)$. The split-place normalization below will also enter the local Hecke calculation of Subsection 6.1.

#thm([Lemma 4.4.])[
  #emph[For the symplectic space $\( bb(V)_P \, psi_P \)$, restriction of the unitary splitting to $G \( bb(A)_F \)$ yields an honest finite-adelic Weil action $omega_(P \, f)$. It is compatible with the rational covariance in Lemma 12 and is independent of the splitting characters. At infinity its half-determinant action is the natural action on $L^n$ in (4.6).]
  #emph[At a split finite place, choose as Schrödinger positions for a multiplicity line one standard three-dimensional embedding summand. The resulting $"SL"_3$-action is inverse linear substitution. More generally, the normalized linear action associated with a transformation $h$ of the position space is 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.15)")
 $ \( rho \( h \) b \) \( x \) = \| det h \|^(- 1 \/ 2) b \( h^(- 1) x \) . $ <eq:theta-linear-action>]

 A bare metaplectic lift outside $"SL"_3$ is the normalized operator $rho \( h \)$ times its lift scalar; that scalar must be retained in any use of such a lift.]
  #emph[Every finite list of inputs is fixed under $omega_(P \, f)$ by all sufficiently deep principal lattice congruence subgroups.]
] <lem:unitary-splitting>

#proof[
  First we check that the required splitting characters exist. For the idele class groups $C_F$ and $C_E$, the natural map $C_F arrow.r C_E$ is injective: if an $F$-idele becomes a principal $E$-idele, its generator is fixed by $c$ and hence belongs to $F$. This map is also proper. The absolute idele norm on $C_E$ restricts to the square of the norm on $C_F$, whose norm-one kernel is compact. A compact subset of $C_E$ has norms in a compact subinterval of $bb(R)_(> 0)$; its preimage is therefore a closed subset of a compact norm interval times the compact norm-one kernel of $C_F$. Thus $C_F$ is a closed locally compact subgroup of $C_E$. The character-extension theorem for a closed subgroup of a locally compact abelian group extends each unitary character $epsilon.alt_(E \/ F)^(thin r)$ from $C_F$ to $C_E$ #cite(label("Folland2015")) §4.3, Corollary 4.42. Apply this with $r = m \, n$.
  The splitting theorem applies to our form by replacing $h_P$ with the skew-Hermitian form $delta h_P$. With both forms linear in the first variable, the trace product in #cite(label("BoradeEtAl2025ToriPeriods")) §§2.1 and 3.1 is exactly (4.4). We take the same global additive character as in Lemma 12. The determinant twists from changing the characters vanish on $G = "SU" \( V \)$. The product of the finite local splittings is therefore an honest action $omega_(P \, f)$ on $G \( bb(A)_(F \, f) \)$, independent of those characters.
  We compare its infinite circle lift with the double lift needed for the differential bundles. At the active maximal compact subgroup $S \( "U" \( 2 \) times "U" \( 1 \) \)$, let $ell$ be the character on the negative line. The determinant of the positive plane is $ell^(- 1)$, so the determinant character on $cal(F)_P$ is $ell^(2 n)$. At every other real place the determinant character is trivial on $"SU" \( 3 \)$. On the maximal compact $"U" \( a \)$ of each real symplectic factor, the metaplectic double cover is the determinant-square-root cover. For the product of the real factors its square is the product of those determinant characters. Its pullback to the maximal compact subgroup of $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$ therefore splits with square-root character $ell^n$.
  A connected real reductive group retracts onto its maximal compact subgroup. The preceding determinant computation says that every fundamental-group loop of $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$ has even determinant winding in the symplectic group. The lift from the universal covering group consequently descends to a group lift of $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$ into the double cover. Its half-determinant action is the one on $L^n$ in (4.6). The original circle lift and this double lift, pushed out to the circle extension, differ by a continuous character of $G \( F ⊗_(bb(Q)) bb(R) \)$. This group is connected and semisimple. The differential of such a character is zero, and connectedness makes the character trivial. The two lifts therefore agree. The rational compatibility of the global splitting now pairs $omega_(P \, f) \( gamma \)$ with this infinite lift for every $gamma in G \( F \)$, proving the claimed rational covariance.
  At a split finite place the two embedding summands are dual stable Lagrangians. If $h$ is the action on the position summand, the symplectic matrix is $"diag" \( h^(- t) \, h \)$. The Schrödinger linear formula gives (4.15), up to the scalar of the chosen bare lift. On $"SL"_3 \( F_v \)$, the normalized substitution lift and the unitary lift have a character as their ratio. This character is trivial because $"SL"_3 \( F_v \)$ is perfect: elementary matrices generate it, and for distinct $i \, j \, k$,
  $ e_(i j) \( u \) = \[ e_(i k) \( u \) \, e_(k j) \( 1 \) \] . $
  The determinant factor is also $1$ there. This proves the split-place assertion without removing scalars from lifts outside $"SL"_3$.
  Finally the finite Weil action is smooth, so finitely many Schwartz functions have a common open stabilizer. Intersect it with the fixed lattice stabilizer in $G \( bb(A)_(F \, f) \)$. Principal lattice congruence neighborhoods form a basis at the identity in that stabilizer, so a sufficiently deep principal one lies in the intersection. This proves the last assertion. ◻
]

Restrict the tensors of Lemma 12 to the period map from $D$. If a finite list of inputs is fixed by an open compact $K_f subset G \( bb(A)_(F \, f) \)$, the lemma just proved and (4.6) give their bundle covariance for $Gamma = G \( F \) ∩ K_f$. Passing to a torsion-free principal subgroup makes all of them descend to one compact quotient. This uses stabilizers in the smooth finite-adelic representation; it makes no claim that an arbitrary finite-index subgroup of a ball lattice is congruence.

=== 4.5 Theta one-forms span cotangent fibers <subsec:theta-one-forms>

For a line $W_i$, project $T_(1 \, phi.alt)$ to the $c mu$ summand of the positive Lagrangian. Using our fixed complex trivialization of the constant line in (4.7), denote the resulting one-form on $D$ by $Theta_i \( phi.alt \)$.

#thm([Proposition 4.5.])[
  #emph[For every finite input $phi.alt$, the form $Theta_i \( phi.alt \)$ is holomorphic on $D$ and descends to every sufficiently deep principal congruence quotient. At any fixed point of $D$, the values $Theta_i \( phi.alt \)$, as $phi.alt$ varies over finite Schwartz inputs, span the cotangent fiber. Finitely many actual inputs already span that fiber and descend at a common sufficiently deep principal level.]
] <prop:theta-one>

#proof[
  Holomorphy follows from that of $T_(1 \, phi.alt)$, the holomorphic embedding projection, and (4.3). Descent follows from Lemma 14.
  Fix a point $x_(*) in D$ and its period point $Z$. Here $a = 3$. For $0 eq.not x_0 in cal(O)_F^a$ and positive integers $k$, take
  $ phi.alt_k = upright(bold(1))_(thin x_0 + k hat(cal(O))_F^(thin a)) . $
  Its rational support is exactly $x_0 + k cal(O)_F^a$. The first Taylor tensor is the ordinary gradient, so in the chosen graph frame
  $ T_(1 \, phi.alt_k) \( Z \) = 2 pi i sum_(x in x_0 + k cal(O)_F^a) \( x_tau \)_(tau in Sigma) exp #h(-0.16667em) (pi i sum_tau x_tau^t Z_tau x_tau) . $
  Under the Minkowski embedding $cal(O)_F^a$ is a full lattice. If $rho > 0$ is the minimum norm of a nonzero lattice vector, then, for $m eq.not 0$ in this lattice and $k gt.eq 2 parallel x_0 parallel \/ rho$,
  $ parallel x_0 + k m parallel gt.eq k parallel m parallel \/ 2 . $
  The Gaussian bound used above, with any polynomial factor absorbed into a weaker Gaussian, bounds the tail by a constant times a convergent lattice sum of the form $sum_(m eq.not 0) exp \( - c k^2 parallel m parallel^2 \)$. That sum tends to zero. The same argument is uniform for $Z$ in a compact neighborhood. We have proved
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.16)")
   $ T_(1 \, phi.alt_k) \( Z \) arrow.r 2 pi i thin \( x_(0 \, tau) \)_(tau in Sigma) exp #h(-0.16667em) (pi i sum_tau x_(0 \, tau)^t Z_tau x_(0 \, tau)) . $ <eq:theta-sparse-limit>]
  The scalar exponential is nonzero. As $x_0$ varies, the vectors $\( x_(0 \, tau) \)_tau$ span the real position space, and their complex span is the entire first-tensor fiber in this frame. The complex span of the actual values $T_(1 \, phi.alt) \( Z \)$ is a subspace of a finite-dimensional space and hence is closed. It contains all the limits in (4.16), so it is the full fiber. Projection onto $\( det cal(F)_i \)^(1 \/ 2) ⊗ \( cal(F)_i \)_(c mu)$ is surjective; therefore the projected values span $Omega_D^1 \|_(x_(*))$. Choose finitely many actual inputs whose values contain a basis, and then apply the stabilizer assertion of Lemma 14.
  The inputs $phi.alt_k$ in this limit need not have a common congruence stabilizer as $k$ varies. The limit takes place in one fiber over $D$; a common level is chosen only after finitely many actual inputs have been selected. ◻
]

On a compact Kähler quotient a holomorphic one-form is closed and harmonic. A nonzero such form therefore has nonzero cohomology class. These are complex classes. Nothing in this proposition identifies a rational CM source for them.

=== 4.6 Rank-two tensors, wedges, and fixed-data variation <subsec:theta-rank-two>

Take $W = W_1 perp W_2$, so $n = 2$. Project $T_(2 \, phi.alt)$ to $"Sym"^2 \( \( cal(F)_W \)_(c mu) \) = "Sym"^2 \( Q^(*) ⊗ W_mu^(*) \)$, and then apply $"alt"_2$. Its target is the canonical bundle in (4.8), up to a constant line. After trivializing that line, denote the resulting differential two-form by $Xi_W \( phi.alt \)$. At a rational decomposition the product formula for theta series will express it as a finite sum of wedges of the one-forms just constructed. We then vary the compact-place data of the total space to compare two such expressions.

We now specify exactly what may vary in this construction. Keep fixed the rational Hermitian space $W$, the symplectic space $\( bb(V)_W \, psi_W \)$, the rational embedding $G arrow.r "Sp" \( bb(V)_W \)$, reference rational Schrödinger coordinates, the global Weil and unitary splitting choices, and the resulting finite action $omega_(W \, f)$. Also fix a single total finite input $phi.alt$ in these reference coordinates. At a compact place $tau eq.not mu$, let

$ W_tau = W_tau^(+) perp W_tau^(-) $

range over orthogonal decompositions with

$ dim W_tau^plus.minus = \# { i in { 1 \, 2 } : d_i \( tau \) = plus.minus 1 } . $

Require the form on $W_tau^(+)$ to have sign $- "sgn" \( t_tau \)$, and the form on $W_tau^(-)$ the opposite sign. Let $cal(T)$ be the product of these parameter spaces. A definite local factor is a point; when the two signs differ, the local factor is the domain of lines of one prescribed sign in a Hermitian plane of signature $\( 1 \, 1 \)$.

For $t in cal(T)$, the two compact-place pieces of the positive Lagrangian are

$ V_tau ⊗ W_tau^(-) \, #h(2em) V_tau^(*) ⊗ \( W_tau^(+) \)^(*) . $

In the second expression $\( W_tau^(+) \)^(*)$ is embedded as $"ann" \( W_tau^(-) \) subset W_tau^(*)$; the orthogonal decomposition identifies it with the dual of $W_tau^(+)$. The active pieces remain, exactly,

$ L ⊗ W_mu \, #h(2em) Q^(*) ⊗ W_mu^(*) . $

They depend only on $W_mu$, not on any decomposition of it. These prescriptions give positive Lagrangians for all $t in cal(T)$. The same projection as in (4.8), with a local smooth choice of constant-line trivialization, defines a smooth form of type $\( 2 \, 0 \)$, denoted $Xi_W \( phi.alt \; t \)$, even when $t$ is not induced by rational lines. We do not claim it is holomorphic for such an intermediate parameter.

#thm([Proposition 4.6.])[
  #emph[The rank-two construction has the following properties.]
  + #emph[At period data induced by a rational orthogonal decomposition $W = W_1 perp W_2$, the form $Xi_W \( phi.alt \)$ is holomorphic. On a sufficiently deep common principal quotient it is a finite sum, with constant coefficients, of forms $Theta_1 \( phi.alt_(1 \, r) \) and Theta_2 \( phi.alt_(2 \, r) \)$.]
  + #emph[There is an input which is a product in adapted rational coordinates and for which $Xi_W \( phi.alt \)$ is nonzero.]
  + #emph[Fix $W$, the total finite input $phi.alt$, and a torsion-free principal quotient $X$ whose finite level fixes $phi.alt$ under $omega_(W \, f)$. With locally smooth constant-line trivializations, the map 
$ t mapsto Xi_W \( phi.alt \; t \) $

 is continuous in the $C^oo$ topology of differential forms on this fixed compact quotient. The same finite level works throughout the parameter neighborhood.]
] <prop:theta-top>

#proof[
  For (i), the rational orthogonal decomposition gives a rational symplectic direct sum $bb(V)_W = bb(V)_(W_1) perp bb(V)_(W_2)$. Choose rational coordinates adapted to it. By Remark 13, the total reference input becomes another finite Schwartz input in the adapted coordinates. Such an input is a finite sum
  $ sum_r phi.alt_(1 \, r) ⊗ phi.alt_(2 \, r) . $
  Indeed, a locally constant compactly supported function on the product of two finite-adelic vector spaces is invariant under a sufficiently small product compact open subgroup. Only finitely many of its cosets meet the support, and each coset is a product coset.
  For a product input the completed theta series multiply, since both the sum and its completion split in the adapted coordinates. If $T_j^(\( i \))$ denotes the degree-$j$ coefficient for the $i$-th line, the coefficient of total degree two is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.17)")
   $ T_2^(\( 1 \)) T_0^(\( 2 \)) + T_1^(\( 1 \)) T_1^(\( 2 \)) + T_0^(\( 1 \)) T_2^(\( 2 \)) . $ <eq:theta-quadratic-product>]
  The coefficient of the mixed product is $1$, by the $1 \/ k !$ normalization in (4.10). The two unmixed terms have both multiplicity factors in the same one-dimensional $\( W_i \)_mu^(*)$; they vanish under $"alt"_2$. The mixed term becomes the wedge of the two projected first tensors. With compatible constant-line trivializations it is exactly $Theta_1 \( phi.alt_(1 \, r) \) and Theta_2 \( phi.alt_(2 \, r) \)$; other trivializations multiply it by a fixed nonzero scalar. Rational changes of coordinates are already accounted for by (4.12). Moreover, two identifications with the differential bundle used here have a ratio invariant under the transitive infinite special-unitary action on $D$, so that ratio is constant. This proves the wedge expansion and its holomorphy. We have proved holomorphy only after the double-alternating projection: the whole completed second Taylor tensor need not be holomorphic. The finitely many individual inputs descend on a further common principal level by Lemma 14.
  For (ii), fix a point of $D$. Proposition 15 provides an input for $W_1$ with a nonzero cotangent value and an input for $W_2$ whose value is outside the line it spans. Their wedge is nonzero at that point. The product of these inputs in adapted coordinates has a nonzero double-alternating second tensor by the calculation above. Transporting this product back to the reference coordinates gives a total finite input with $Xi_W \( phi.alt \) eq.not 0$.
  For (iii), no rational coordinate change is made as $t$ varies. The rational symplectic space, its rational $G$-action, the finite representation, and the total input all remain fixed. The changed compact-place Lagrangians remain invariant under $"SU" \( 3 \)$, since all changes are in the multiplicity factor. Their determinant characters on $"SU" \( 3 \)$ are trivial. The active pieces have not changed, so their determinant character remains $ell^4$. The infinite comparison in Lemma 14 therefore gives the same half-determinant action $L^2$ at every $t$. Consequently the same subgroup fixing $phi.alt$ gives equivariance and descent at every parameter. There is no finite translation, Hecke choice, or change of level in this variation.
  It remains to check uniformity on the quotient. The constant determinant factors in (4.6) vary smoothly with the compact-place subspaces. On a small parameter neighborhood choose smooth nonzero frames and their square roots, agreeing with the chosen frames at the center. Cover the fixed compact $X$ by finitely many charts with precompact lifts to $D$. On the closure of each lifted chart times a sufficiently small compact parameter neighborhood, the period map is smooth, its image is a compact subset of the Siegel domain, and every fixed order of its derivatives is bounded. The imaginary parts thus have a common positive lower bound. The rational support of the fixed $phi.alt$ continues to lie in one fixed finite union of lattice cosets. The Gaussian estimates following (4.10) now give uniform convergence of every fixed order of derivatives in the chart coordinates and the parameters. Projection to (4.8) and the chosen smooth trivializations preserve these bounds. The unchanged equivariance glues these convergent local expressions on $X$. This is continuity in the $C^oo$ topology. Compactness of $X$ is used here; no estimate at a cusp or boundary is needed. ◻
]

=== 4.7 Approximation while the total theta input stays fixed <subsec:theta-approximation>

We now apply the last proposition to the sign switch. The approximation occurs in $cal(T)$, before the rational coordinate changes used to factor either endpoint.

#thm([Corollary 4.7.])[
  #emph[Suppose $d_1 + d_2 = d_3 + d_4$, with $d_i \( mu \) = 1$ for all $i$. Start with $W = W_1 perp W_2$ as above, let $t_0$ be its compact-place data, and choose a total input $phi.alt$ for which $xi = Xi_W \( phi.alt \; t_0 \)$ is a nonzero holomorphic top form on a sufficiently deep compact principal quotient $X$. There are rational orthogonal decompositions $W = W_3 perp W_4$ with line signs $d_3 \, d_4$ whose total compact-place positive Lagrangians are arbitrarily close to those at $t_0$. The total active positive Lagrangian is exactly unchanged.]
  #emph[For a sufficiently close rational choice $t'$, the form $xi' = Xi_W \( phi.alt \; t' \)$ is holomorphic on the same quotient $X$, with the same total finite input, and 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.18)")
 $ integral_X xi and overline(xi') eq.not 0 . $ <eq:theta-nonzero-pairing>]

 Both endpoints admit the wedge expansions of Proposition 16 on a further common principal cover, and their pairing remains nonzero there.]
] <cor:theta-switch-continuity>

#proof[
  At a compact place $tau eq.not mu$, equality of the two sign sums says exactly that the multisets ${ d_1 \( tau \) \, d_2 \( tau \) }$ and ${ d_3 \( tau \) \, d_4 \( tau \) }$ coincide. Reorder the two original local lines so that the first has the sign required by $d_3 \( tau \)$, and choose a nonzero vector on it. After fixing an $E$-basis of $W$, weak approximation in $E$, coordinate by coordinate at these finitely many complex places, gives a single $w in W \( E \)$ arbitrarily close to all the chosen local vectors. Nondegeneracy and the signs of their Hermitian norms are open conditions. For a sufficiently close approximation,
  $ W_3 = E w \, #h(2em) W_4 = W_3^perp $
  is therefore a rational orthogonal decomposition. Its first line has the prescribed compact-place signs for $d_3$; orthogonal inertia gives the complementary signs for $d_4$. At $mu$ the form on $W_mu$ is definite, so both new lines automatically have the same required sign there.
  The total compact-place Lagrangians converge to the original ones. Reordering the original lines only relabels their total positive summands. For a nondegenerate local vector the orthogonal projection onto its line is
  $ z mapsto h_W \( z \, w \) h_W \( w \, w \)^(- 1) w . $
  It varies continuously with $w$, so both the line and its orthogonal complement converge to the chosen original ones. The selected signed subspaces, and hence the total positive Lagrangians, converge. At the active pair the total pieces are always $L ⊗ W_mu$ and $Q^(*) ⊗ W_mu^(*)$, so they are unchanged, not merely close. The approximating rational lines may have small active norms or large arithmetic denominators. No bound on these is needed: the active total period data are fixed, and the continuity assertion concerns the total tensor in the original reference coordinates.
  All the hypotheses of Proposition 16(iii) now hold with the same rational $W$, the same $G$, the same total finite $phi.alt$, and the same compact $X$. Thus
  $ Xi_W \( phi.alt \; t' \) arrow.r Xi_W \( phi.alt \; t_0 \) $
  smoothly on $X$. The limit $xi$ is a nonzero holomorphic top form. In local complex coordinates $xi = f thin d z_1 and d z_2$, the form $xi and macron(xi)$ is a positive multiple of $\| f \|^2 thin d x_1 and d y_1 and d x_2 and d y_2$. Consequently $integral_X xi and macron(xi) > 0$. Continuity of the integral gives (4.18) for all sufficiently close rational choices. At each such endpoint holomorphy follows from Proposition 16(i), not from a holomorphy assertion along the intermediate parameter path.
  Factor the two endpoints only now. There are finitely many line inputs in their two wedge expansions. Choose a principal subgroup contained both in the level of $X$ and in their finite stabilizers. It gives a finite unramified cover $pi : X' arrow.r X$ on which all individual factors descend. The covering formula is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.19)")
   $ integral_(X') pi^(*) xi and overline(pi^(*) xi') = deg \( pi \) integral_X xi and overline(xi') eq.not 0 . $ <eq:theta-cover-pairing>]
  Both quotients are compact and torsion-free, so there is no cusp term or ramification correction. This proves the last assertion. ◻
]

Expansion of the two pulled-back endpoint forms on $X'$ in (4.19) gives (4.1); in particular at least one pair of individual wedge terms has nonzero mixed period. This is the analytic nonvanishing needed for Proposition 9. To apply that criterion we must still express the relevant one-form classes as complex linear combinations of images of the selected CM lines under rational Hodge maps, on a common projective principal cover. That arithmetic step is separate from the approximation just proved.

== 5 Projective ball quotients and a Frobenius identity <sec:arithmetic>

The theta construction has supplied complex one-form classes on compact ball quotients. To use the surface criterion, we must identify their rational sources: for the sign function $v$, the relevant class must lie in the complex span of the images of $U \( v \)_1$ under rational Hodge maps from $U \( v \)$. This section and Section 6 prove the following extraction theorem. We first recall its input data from the theta construction.

Retain the notation of Section 4. Thus $E subset overline(bb(Q))$ is Galois CM, $F = E^(+)$, $G_E = "Gal" \( E \/ bb(Q) \)$, and $c in G_E$ is complex conjugation. We assume $s = \[ F : bb(Q) \] gt.eq 2$. The Hermitian $E \/ F$-space $V$ has dimension three, signature $\( 2 \, 1 \)$ at $mu = 1$, and is positive definite at the other real places. Write $G = "SU" \( V \)$, let $D$ be its ball of negative lines, and retain the fixed $cal(O)_E$-lattice $Lambda subset V$. Fix an odd function $v : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$ with $v \( 1 \) = 1$, and put

$ d \( tau \) = v \( tau^(- 1) \) . $

For one multiplicity line $P$ with these compact-place signs, write $Theta \( phi.alt \)$ for its theta one-form and abbreviate its finite special-unitary action to $omega_f = omega_(P \, f)$. We work with a finite collection of inputs at a common principal level. The auxiliary prime will be chosen only after that level is fixed.

#thm([Theorem 5.1.])[
  (Degree-one CM extraction).  #emph[Let $v : G_E arrow.r { 1 \, - 1 }$ be odd with $v \( 1 \) = 1$, and use the multiplicity-line theta construction prescribed by $d \( tau \) = v \( tau^(- 1) \)$. Given a finite collection of its finite adelic Schwartz inputs, there is a common sufficiently deep principal ball quotient $S_0 = Gamma without D$ on which every associated theta one-form class belongs to 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
 $ "span"_(bb(C)) {f #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] U \( v \)_1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] : f in "Hom"_(upright("Hdg")) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] U \( v \) \, H^1 \( S_0 \, bb(Q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]} . $ <eq:cm-extraction-span>]

 The maps in this formula are rational Hodge maps, and their images are viewed after extension to $bb(C)$. The conclusion persists after passage to a deeper principal level.]
] <thm:cm-extraction>

The quotient $S_0$ will be a smooth connected projective surface. Corollary 29 gives the version needed for the surface criterion: finitely many families with different sign functions satisfy these span conclusions on one projective principal cover of any prescribed torsion-free principal base quotient. The reference moduli construction depends only on $V$, so the same geometry serves every multiplicity line.

We begin by realizing the principal quotients as components of a full projective moduli space of polarized abelian varieties with $E$-action. Keeping all components makes the isogeny correspondences into algebraic Hecke operators on the product of their Albanese varieties. Dense ordinary loci then let us prove an integral Frobenius polynomial for these operators. Section 6 combines that polynomial with the active theta parameter to determine the Hodge types of the generalized Hecke spaces containing the theta classes. Scalar descent then proves the extraction theorem.

=== 5.1 A projective moduli space for the reference three-space <subsec:reference-moduli>

The reference abelian varieties use $V$, not $V ⊗_E P$. For a negative line $L in D$, prescribe on the complexified homology

$ cal(F)_mu = L \, #h(2em) cal(F)_c = "ann" \( L \) subset V_mu^(*) \, $

where the Hermitian form identifies the conjugate summand with the complex-linear dual. At each other conjugate pair choose, once and for all, the whole summand on one side and zero on the other. These annihilator prescriptions together form a Lagrangian. An imaginary element $delta_(upright("ref")) in E^times$, whose signs are chosen by weak approximation in $F$, makes them positive for

$ psi_(upright("ref")) \( x \, y \) = "Tr"_(E \/ bb(Q)) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] delta_(upright("ref")) h_V \( x \, y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

Multiplying $delta_(upright("ref"))$ by a positive integer makes the form integral on $Lambda$.

Here the homology filtration is the kernel of the Lie quotient. If $J$ is the complex structure on real homology, then $z = u + i J u$ lies in that filtration and

$ psi_(upright("ref")) \( z \, overline(z) \) \/ i = - 2 psi_(upright("ref")) \( u \, J u \) . $

Thus $- psi_(upright("ref"))$ is the Riemann form in the positivity convention of Section 4. The corresponding abelian varieties have dimension $3 s$, and their Lie ranks are

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ r_mu = 2 \, #h(2em) r_c = 1 \, #h(2em) { r_tau \, r_(c tau) } = { 0 \, 3 } quad #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] tau in.not { 1 \, c } #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <eq:reference-lie-ranks>]

Fix their polarization type.

Choose $N$ sufficiently divisible to give a fine abelian moduli problem, a torsion-free principal lattice level, and a level fixing the chosen finite theta inputs. We will also require the level to kill all possible unitary determinant roots of unity. A marking means a full ordered basis of $N$-torsion; we allow every pairing matrix for that basis. In particular, transporting it through an isogeny of degree prime to $N$ is allowed even when the isogeny rescales the polarization pairing.

We retain all components so that the full isogeny correspondences can be defined without assuming that the reference component is preserved. Over a number field $K_0 subset overline(bb(Q))$ containing $E$, consider the reduced scheme $S$ of tuples

$ \( B \, lambda \, iota_E \, eta_N \) . $

Here $B$ is an abelian variety of dimension $3 s$, $lambda$ is a polarization of the fixed type, $iota_E : cal(O)_E arrow.r "End" \( B \)$ is a unital action whose Rosati adjoint is $c$, $eta_N$ is the full marking, and each Lie summand has the particular rank fixed above (with values displayed in (5.2)). We retain all connected components of this tuple space.

#thm([Lemma 5.2.])[
  #emph[The scheme $S$ is of finite type, smooth, and projective over $K_0$. Its complex connected components are compact ball quotients. For sufficiently divisible $N$, the component arising from $Lambda$ is the quotient of $D$ by its principal special-unitary level.]
] <lem:reference-moduli>

#proof[
  The Lie-rank condition need not identify a single rational Hermitian isomorphism class. We therefore construct the whole finite-type action locus, identify its complex components as ball quotients, and then prove projectivity and identify the component of $Lambda$.
  #emph[The finite-type action locus.] The fine-moduli theorem for polarized abelian varieties gives a quasi-projective scheme with universal polarization and full level at sufficiently large invertible level #cite(label("MumfordFogarty1982")) Chapter 7, Definitions 7.1–7.2, Proposition 7.6, and Theorem 7.9. In that theorem the polarization degree is fixed and the level is an ordered torsion basis, with no prescribed pairing matrix. After inverting the polarization degree, the polarization kernel is finite étale, so its geometric elementary-divisor type is locally constant. We may therefore take the open-and-closed part of the required type. If necessary, first apply the theorem to a sufficiently large divisor of $N$ prime to the polarization degree and then add the remaining full $N$-basis as a finite étale level cover. These constructions work over a ring of integers with finitely many primes inverted, as well as in characteristic zero.
  We next impose the action by a finite-type construction. Choose finitely many ring generators $b$ of $cal(O)_E$ and parameterize their graphs in relative Hilbert schemes of the square of the universal abelian scheme. The degrees of the graphs in question are bounded. Indeed, put $alpha = 1 + macron(b) b$. It is a nonzero totally positive element of $F$. Choose a positive integer $m$ with $m alpha^(- 1) in cal(O)_E$. In every such action $alpha$ is an isogeny, and
  $ deg \( alpha \) deg \( m alpha^(- 1) \) = m^(6 s) . $
  The second factor is a positive integer, so $deg \( alpha \)$ is bounded independently of the point.
  To translate this into a Hilbert bound, the normalized Poincaré bundle on the universal abelian scheme times its dual, pulled back by $\( "id" \, lambda \)$, is a relatively ample bundle inducing $2 lambda$. The tensor product of its two pullbacks to the square restricts on the graph of $b$ to a bundle inducing $2 lambda \( 1 + macron(b) b \)$. Its polarization degree is bounded by the preceding identity. Riemann–Roch on an abelian variety of dimension $3 s$ gives the Hilbert polynomial $chi \( cal(L) \) n^(3 s)$, with $chi \( cal(L) \)^2 = deg \( phi.alt_(cal(L)) \)$. Only finitely many polynomials can therefore occur. On their graph loci impose the homomorphism, ring, Rosati, and Lie-rank identities. This gives a finite-type action locus.
  Over the characteristic-zero polarized base this locus is unramified. One form of rigidity suffices: an infinitesimal difference of two homomorphisms between fixed abelian schemes would give a homomorphism from a proper connected abelian fiber to an additive tangent group, and must vanish. The locus is also proper over that base. Given a discrete valuation ring mapping to the polarized base and a lift of its generic point to the action locus, each generator homomorphism extends uniquely between the fixed abelian schemes by the Néron mapping property #cite(label("BoschLutkebohmertRaynaud1990")) §1.2, Definition 1 and Proposition 8. Its graph remains a graph of the same Hilbert polynomial. The ring and Rosati identities extend by uniqueness. Since the base contains $E$, the action algebra splits into idempotents in characteristic zero, and their Lie summands have locally constant ranks. Thus the extended point lies in the same action locus. The valuative criterion gives properness. A proper finite-type unramified morphism is quasi-finite and hence finite, so the action locus is finite over the polarized base.
  #emph[The complex components.] We now identify the reduced complex structure of this algebraic locus. In a marked period chart, the integral matrices of the action are locally constant. Such a matrix acts on the complex torus exactly when it preserves the homology filtration; the resulting holomorphic endomorphism of a polarized complex torus is algebraic. Hence the reduced local locus is precisely the locus of positive filtrations stable under the fixed matrices. Trace duality and the Rosati identity express the alternating form as an imaginary trace multiple of an $E \/ F$-Hermitian form on the rational homology, of $E$-dimension three. Isotropy makes the two filtrations in a conjugate pair annihilators. Positivity and (5.2) leave one negative line at the active place and constant data at every other place. The local locus is therefore a smooth complex two-ball.
  This description gives whole ball quotients, not only local ball charts. Fix a marked polarized $E$-lattice occurring in $S$. Every positive filtration in its compatible ball gives a complex torus with the fixed integral Riemann form, and hence an abelian variety; the preserving integral matrices give its algebraic $cal(O)_E$-action. Conversely every tuple with these lattice data gives such a filtration. Its connected component is thus the quotient of the whole ball by the marked lattice stabilizer.
  #emph[Projectivity and the principal level.] For each such rational Hermitian space, a vector isotropic over $E$ would remain isotropic at a definite real place. Since $s gt.eq 2$, there is no such vector, and its special-unitary group is $F$-anisotropic. The arithmetic lattice and compactness theorems give compact ball quotients #cite(label("BorelHarishChandra1962")); see #cite(label("MilneShimura2017")) Theorem 3.3 and Proposition 3.5. The level removes stabilizers. There are finitely many components because $S$ is of finite type. The finite action locus is quasi-projective, and its reduced complex space is smooth with compact components. It is therefore proper, and a proper quasi-projective scheme is projective. Smoothness and properness descend from the complex base change to $K_0$.
  Finally the initial lattice $Lambda$ is among these data. Even if it is not free, its determinant module is a fractional $cal(O)_E$-ideal. The determinant of a unitary lattice automorphism is consequently a unit of $cal(O)_E$ of norm one. All its complex conjugates have modulus one, so it is a root of unity. A sufficiently divisible principal level kills every nontrivial such determinant. Its stabilizer is then precisely the principal special-unitary lattice level, as claimed. ◻
]

=== 5.2 Good models and ordinary points on every component <subsec:good-ordinary-models>

Enlarge $K_0$ to a number field $K subset overline(bb(Q))$ over which all connected components $S_j$ are geometrically connected and have $K$-points. Index the original ball component by $S_0$. Use those points as origins for the Albanese maps

$ a_j : S_j arrow.r A_j = "Alb" \( S_j \) \, #h(2em) A_S = product_j A_j . $

We regard each $A_j$ as a factor of $A_S$, with zero in the other factors. The Albanese pullbacks give an isomorphism of rational Hodge structures

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
 $ H^1 \( A_S \, bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \) . $ <eq:albanese-h1>]

Because the Frobenius identity will be checked on ordinary points and then on Albanese differences, we need density on every component and generation of each reduced Albanese factor by those differences.

#thm([Lemma 5.3.])[
  #emph[After one finite extension of $K$ and removal of finitely many primes, the $S_j$ have smooth proper models with geometrically connected fibers, the $A_j$ extend to abelian schemes, and the $a_j$ extend to the models. The model of $S$ lies in the full tuple parameter scheme, retaining the $cal(O)_E$-action and its graph coordinates. Differences of Albanese images generate every geometric fiber of each $A_j$.]
  #emph[At every remaining rational prime $q$ completely split in $K$, the ordinary locus of the universal abelian scheme is dense in each geometric component of the special fiber of $S$.]
] <lem:good-ordinary-primes>

#proof[
  #emph[Models and Albanese generation.] Spread the finitely many schemes, morphisms, and universal data over an open part of the ring of integers of $K$. One can obtain the proper model of $S$ by taking its closure in a projective compactification of the full parameter scheme used in the proof of Lemma 19. Its intersection with the boundary has proper closed image on the integer base. That image misses the generic point because $S$ is proper and lies in the parameter scheme. Removing the finitely many primes in the image leaves the closure inside the full parameter scheme. Thus its geometric points are still distinguished by their polarized, acted-on, marked tuples, including the graph coordinates. Further shrinking gives smooth proper models with geometrically connected component fibers, good abelian models, and the extended Albanese maps.
  For a component $S_j$, let $Delta_j = a_j \( S_j \) - a_j \( S_j \) subset A_j$. It is closed, irreducible, symmetric, and contains zero. The successive sum images $n Delta_j$ are nested irreducible closed sets. Their dimensions eventually stabilize; then equality of two successive sets implies stability under addition by $Delta_j$. Symmetry shows that the stable image is the abelian subvariety generated by $Delta_j$, which is $A_j$ by the Albanese property. Thus for some fixed $n$ the proper sum map
  $ \( S_j times S_j \)^n arrow.r A_j $
  is surjective. Spread this map together with the other data. Its closed image contains the generic fiber of the flat abelian scheme; that fiber is dense, so the image is the whole abelian scheme. Consequently the sum map is surjective on every geometric fiber.
  #emph[Ordinary points.] For each characteristic-zero component, we now choose a point whose reduction at the split primes retained below is ordinary. In its rational Hermitian three-space choose, by weak approximation, an $E$-line negative at $mu$, and diagonalize its orthogonal complement. At the period whose negative line is this coordinate line, the three rational orthogonal projectors split the rational homology into three $E$-lines and preserve the filtration. Integral multiples of them are endomorphisms of the corresponding abelian variety.
  These points and endomorphisms are defined over $overline(bb(Q))$. Indeed, add their graphs as extra parameters with the Hilbert polynomials at the selected point. This is a finite-type parameter scheme over a number field. Locally the projector matrices are fixed by rigidity. A negative line preserved by all three projectors must be the unique negative coordinate line, so the selected enhanced point is isolated. An isolated complex point of a finite-type scheme over a number field has algebraic coordinates. Adjoin the finitely many fields of definition to $K$, spread these points and endomorphisms, and exclude their projector and isogeny denominators.
  Now let $q$ be a remaining completely split prime. At each selected point the integral projectors and the idempotents of $cal(O)_E ⊗ bb(Z)_q$ split the $q$-divisible group into pieces of height one. Their heights are the corresponding characteristic-zero Tate ranks and stay constant under specialization. A height-one $q$-divisible group over an algebraically closed field has dimension zero or one. In the first case it is étale; in the second its dual has dimension zero, so it is multiplicative. Thus each selected reduction is ordinary. Ordinariness is open, and a smooth geometrically connected fiber is geometrically irreducible. This nonempty open is dense on each component. Chebotarev’s theorem supplies infinitely many completely split rational primes after the finite exclusions. ◻
]

Choose such a rational prime $q$, also prime to the polarization degree, $N$, the discriminant of $E$, and all exceptional local data of the finite theta inputs. Fix an embedding

$ iota : overline(bb(Q)) arrow.r.hook overline(bb(Q))_q $

and normalize $nu_iota$ by $nu_iota \( q \) = 1$. The resulting completion of $K$ is $bb(Q)_q$, with residue field $bb(F)_q$. For $tau in G_E$, let $frak(p)_tau$ be the prime of $E$ defined by $iota compose tau$. Thus for $x in E^times$,

$ nu_iota \( tau \( x \) \) = "val"_(frak(p)_tau) \( x \) . $

Select one prime from each conjugate pair, including

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
 $ w = frak(p)_c \, #h(2em) macron(w) = frak(p)_1 . $ <eq:active-prime>]

Write $cal(J)$ for this set of selected blocks. The $w$-block has height three and Lie rank one; every other selected block has height three and Lie rank zero or three. The opposite blocks are their polarization duals. These rank assertions persist on the model because the corresponding idempotent Lie and torsion summands are locally free. Choosing $w = frak(p)_c$, rather than $frak(p)_1$, will fix the conjugate-valuation signs.

=== 5.3 Algebraic Hecke operators on the Albanese <subsec:algebraic-hecke>

Choose bases for the selected $q$-adic lattices and the opposite dual lattices. Since the polarization is prime to $q$, the split local similitude group and its integral subgroup are

$ cal(G)_q = (product_(j in cal(J)) "GL"_3 \( bb(Q)_q \)) times bb(Q)_q^times \, #h(2em) cal(K)_q = (product_(j in cal(J)) "GL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)) times bb(Z)_q^times . $

The last coordinate is the common polarization multiplier. Use the raw spherical Hecke algebra with $"vol" \( cal(K)_q \) = 1$. Its integral basis consists of double-coset characteristic functions; its rational extension will act on the Albanese up to isogeny. A double coset is specified by a multiplier exponent $h in bb(Z)$ and, on each selected block, an unordered triple of elementary exponents $\( a_(j \, 1) \, a_(j \, 2) \, a_(j \, 3) \)$. For a representative $g_j$ with those exponents, our target position is the overlattice $g_j^(- 1) Lambda_j$; branches are represented modulo the integral subgroup on the left.

First suppose that

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
 $ h gt.eq 0 \, #h(2em) 0 lt.eq a_(j \, k) lt.eq h . $ <eq:positive-hecke-range>]

On the $j$-block of $B \[ q^h \]$, choose a subgroup of the indicated elementary divisors. On the opposite block choose its annihilator under the perfect $q^h$-torsion pairing. The total subgroup $H$ is maximal isotropic: each conjugate pair contributes order $q^(3 h)$, so its total order is $q^(3 s h)$. Its quotient $f : B arrow.r B' = B \/ H$ is required to have polarization

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
 $ f^or lambda' f = q^h lambda . $ <eq:hecke-polarization>]

We verify that this is an algebraic construction in families, including the polarization homomorphism.

Over the characteristic-zero parameter scheme the torsion and the subgroups are finite étale. The translation quotient exists as an fppf scheme quotient for the free finite-locally-free action #cite(label("StacksProject")) Tags 07S6 and 07S7. Its group law descends, and the finite étale quotient of the abelian scheme is again an abelian scheme.

On a complex fiber the polarization in (5.6) is seen directly on lattices. If $Lambda_j subset Lambda'_j subset q^(- h) Lambda_j$ is the selected target overlattice and the multiplier is $q^h$, its opposite lattice is

$ q^(- h) \( Lambda'_j \)^or . $

The $q^h$-scaled alternating form is integral and perfect on this pair at $q$, by the annihilator condition. At every other prime the $q$-isogeny changes no elementary-divisor valuation. The scaled form is positive, so it gives a Riemann polarization on the quotient of the original type. More generally, the field-level isotropic descent criterion gives the unique symmetric polarization homomorphism on every geometric fiber #cite(label("EdixhovenVanDerGeerMoonen2011Polarizations")) Chapter XI, Proposition 11.25.

Here is the relative descent from this fiber calculation. Since $H subset B \[ q^h \]$, multiplication by $q^h$ descends to $g : B' arrow.r B$, with

$ g f = \[ q^h \]_B \, #h(2em) f g = \[ q^h \]_(B') . $

Put $beta = g^or lambda g : B' arrow.r B' ""^or$. On each geometric fiber the preceding polarization satisfies $beta = q^h lambda'$, so $beta$ kills $B' \[ q^h \]$. The correspondence parameter scheme is smooth and reduced, and $B' \[ q^h \]$ is finite étale over it, hence reduced. Vanishing on its geometric points is therefore vanishing of the relative morphism. The fppf quotient by $B' \[ q^h \]$ now factors $beta$ uniquely through $\[ q^h \]_(B')$. It gives a relative homomorphism $lambda'$ with $beta = q^h lambda'$. Its fibers are the symmetric polarizations just constructed, and cancellation of isogenies gives (5.6). This also proves uniqueness. We have descended the polarization homomorphism, not a particular line bundle.

The $cal(O)_E$-action descends because $H$ is stable. Cancelling $f$ in (5.6) shows that its Rosati adjoint remains $c$. The characteristic-zero Lie ranks are unchanged, and $f$ transports the full $N$-basis. Thus the quotient is a point of the same full tuple space. The subgroup choices form a finite étale scheme over $S$; the quotient construction is its algebraic target map. Distinct kernels remain distinct branches even when their target tuples are isomorphic.

#thm([Lemma 5.4.])[
  #emph[The rational spherical Hecke algebra of $\( cal(G)_q \, cal(K)_q \)$ acts in $"End"_K \( A_S \) ⊗ bb(Q)$ and, by pullback, on $H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \)$. A basis operator in the positive range (5.5) is an integral homomorphism defined over $K$. Its pullback on one-forms is the sum of pullbacks over its target branches.]
] <lem:hecke-albanese>

#proof[
  Let $C arrow.r S$ be one of the finite étale source correspondences. On a source component, compose each target branch with the appropriate Albanese map and sum in $A_S$. This sum descends from a cover trivializing the branches, because it is invariant under their permutations. It is a morphism $n_j : S_j arrow.r A_S$. Subtracting $n_j$ at the basepoint and applying the Albanese universal property gives an integral homomorphism $A_j arrow.r A_S$. On differences it has the exact formula
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.7)")
   $ T #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] a_j \( x \) - a_j \( y \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = sum_(z in C_x) a_(j \( z \)) \( t \( z \) \) - sum_(z in C_y) a_(j \( z \)) \( t \( z \) \) \, $ <eq:hecke-albanese-differences>]
  where $t$ is the target map, $j \( z \)$ indexes the component containing $t \( z \)$, fibers are counted with their branches, and $a_(j \( z \))$ is included in $A_S$. These homomorphisms on the factors define the operator on $A_S$. Their pullback on one-forms is the branch sum, since a constant translation contributes no differential.
  Composition has the raw spherical convolution multiplicities. For a selected lattice $Lambda_j$, a branch is an overlattice $g_j^(- 1) Lambda_j$ with a representative modulo the integral subgroup on the left. If its cumulative multiplier is $q^h$, the opposite target lattice is forced to be $q^(- h) \( Lambda'_j \)^or$. Consequently a chain of quotient choices is precisely a chain of selected overlattices, as in spherical convolution. The opposite lattices, polarizations, and transported markings agree for the same composite. The numbers of branches landing in each target component are locally constant on a connected source. Thus the component basepoint translations cancel when (5.7) is composed.
  Consider the central position with all selected exponents equal to one and multiplier exponent two. It is quotient by $B \[ q \]$; under the multiplication-by-$q$ identification its target has the same polarization and action and marking $q eta_N$. It is therefore a finite-order marking automorphism, since $q$ is invertible modulo $N$. Adding a sufficiently large power of this position to any integer exponent position puts it in (5.5). Define its operator by clearing that power with the inverse marking automorphism. The positive convolution identity shows that this definition is independent of the clearing power. This gives the full rational spherical action. The spherical algebra is commutative, as follows also from the Satake isomorphism #cite(label("Gross1998")) §3. ◻
]

=== 5.4 A Frobenius polynomial from ordinary neighbors <subsec:frobenius-neighbors>

The Hecke action is now algebraic. We next relate its reduction to Frobenius by counting quotient isogenies at ordinary points of the universal family. This calculation depends on the reference three-space and its Lie ranks; it is independent of the theta multiplicity line.

For $i = 1 \, 2 \, 3$, let $T_i$ be the full moduli Hecke operator with $i$ active exponents equal to one, all other selected exponents zero, and multiplier exponent zero. These are the active minuscule cosets in the full similitude algebra. They need not be positive in (5.5); Lemma 21 defines them by central clearing.

Let $C_0$ be the central position with multiplier exponent one and all active exponents zero. On a nonactive selected block, take all three exponents to be one if its Lie rank is three, and zero if its Lie rank is zero. Put

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.8)")
 $ D_i = C_0^i T_i \, #h(2em) 1 lt.eq i lt.eq 3 . $ <eq:integral-D-operators>]

Here $D_i$ has multiplier exponent $i$, active exponents zero or one, and nonactive selected exponents zero or $i$. It is a positive raw operator, hence an integral homomorphism of $A_S$. The quotient branches defining $D_i$ can therefore be studied by specializing the isogenies constructed in Subsection 5.3.

Let $tilde(A)_S \/ bb(F)_q$ be the good reduction at the fixed completion of $K$, and let $Phi$ be its $q$-power Frobenius endomorphism. The $K$-defined integral raw Hecke homomorphisms extend over $bb(Z)_q$ by the Néron property; rational combinations extend after tensoring the endomorphism ring with $bb(Q)$. Their reductions are defined over $bb(F)_q$, so they commute with $Phi$. Use the same letters $D_i$ for these reductions.

#thm([Proposition 5.5.])[
  #emph[In the integral endomorphism ring of $tilde(A)_S$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.9)")
 $ Phi^3 - D_1 Phi^2 + q D_2 Phi - q^3 D_3 = 0 . $ <eq:albanese-frobenius-polynomial>]

]
] <prop:albanese-congruence>

#proof[
  Let $k = overline(bb(F)_q)$, and let $x$ be an ordinary geometric point of the special fiber of $S$. Its active $q$-divisible group has the canonical splitting
  $ G_w = M_0 xor E_0 \, $
  where $M_0$ is multiplicative of height one and $E_0$ is étale of height two. Over the perfect field $k$, the reduced subgroup at each finite torsion level is the canonical étale part; it is preserved by the endomorphism idempotents. Each nonactive selected block is wholly multiplicative or wholly étale according to its Lie rank.
  Three quotient constructions will account for the neighbors. Write $X_0$ for the quotient with active kernel $M_0 \[ q \]$ and multiplier $q$, $Y_0$ for the sum of quotients with active kernel a line $ell subset E_0 \[ q \]$ and multiplier $q$, and $Z_0$ for the quotient with active kernel $E_0 \[ q \]$ and multiplier $q^2$. For multiplier $q^h$, prescribe the connected $q^h$-torsion on every nonactive selected block and the forced annihilator on every opposite block. We will prove that these quotients define full tuples of $S$, that $X_0$ is Frobenius, and that the specialized neighbor sums are
  $ D_1 : med X_0 + q Y_0 \, #h(2em) D_2 : med X_0 Y_0 + q^2 Z_0 \, #h(2em) D_3 : med X_0 Z_0 . $
  The displayed formulas are the identities to establish; at this point the quotient constructions have not yet been identified with operations on $S$. Once they are, their cancellation will give the asserted polynomial on Albanese differences.
