  The active kernels of $D_i$ are $i$-dimensional subspaces of the generic $q$-torsion. The preceding count gives:
  $ upright("operator") & upright("special active kernel") & upright("multiplicity")\
D_1 & M_0 \[ q \] & 1\
 & ell subset E_0 \[ q \] \, med dim_(bb(F)_q) ell = 1 & q upright(" for each ") ell\
D_2 & M_0 \[ q \] xor ell \, med dim_(bb(F)_q) ell = 1 & 1 upright(" for each ") ell\
 & E_0 \[ q \] & q^2\
D_3 & M_0 \[ q \] xor E_0 \[ q \] & 1 . $
  In the first two cases the total number is $q^2 + q + 1$: there are $q + 1$ étale lines. The nonactive selected kernels are uniquely their connected $q^i$-torsion for $D_i$.
  #emph[Full tuples and Frobenius.] We now show that the counted kernels give targets in $S$, and identify the resulting quotient operations. Properness of the model of $S$ extends each generic neighbor over a finite valuation-ring extension. The universal source and target are abelian schemes over that new DVR. Apply the Néron mapping property there, anew, to extend the generic isogeny #cite(label("BoschLutkebohmertRaynaud1990")) §1.2, Proposition 8. The polarization identity extends by separatedness. On each fiber the kernel of the extension is contained in the finite kernel of $f^or lambda' f = \[ q^i \] lambda$, so it has finite kernel and is an isogeny between equal-dimensional abelian varieties. Since source and target polarizations have the same degree, its degree is $q^(3 s i)$ on every fiber. Properness and fiberwise quasi-finiteness make the extension finite. Fiberwise flatness of isogenies between the smooth fibers and the relative flatness criterion make it flat; see #cite(label("BoschLutkebohmertRaynaud1990")) §7.3, Lemma 1 and Proposition 6; §2.4, Proposition 2. Its finite flat kernel is therefore the schematic closure of its generic kernel.
  Finite-locally-free Cartier duality commutes with base change. For a subgroup $H subset G$ in a finite group with a perfect pairing, the annihilator is
  $ "ann" \( H \) = ker \( G^D arrow.r H^D \) = \( G \/ H \)^D . $
