  Frobenius commutes with $Y_0$ and with $Z_0$ as multiset operations. For any of their quotient maps $f : B arrow.r B'$,
  $ F_(B') f = f^(\( q \)) F_B . $
  Frobenius is an isomorphism on the étale part and permutes its line choices bijectively; this square also respects the polarizations, actions, and markings. On the active block the composite $X_0 Y_0$ has kernel $M_0 \[ q \] xor ell$ with multiplier $q^2$, and $X_0 Z_0$ has kernel $M_0 \[ q \] xor E_0 \[ q \]$ with multiplier $q^3$.
  For clarity, the opposite kernels of these composites are indeed forced by their selected kernels and multipliers. On the $q$-divisible groups, write $G \, G'$ for source and target and define the polarization adjoint $f^dagger = lambda^(- 1) f^D lambda'$; the polarizations are isomorphisms there because their degrees are prime to $q$. For a composite with multiplier $q^h$,
  $ f^dagger f = \[ q^h \] \, #h(2em) f f^dagger = \[ q^h \] . $
