For the cohomological application, we record the Frobenius convention. On $H_(upright("cris"))^1 \( tilde(A)_S \/ bb(Z)_q \) \[ 1 \/ q \]$, crystalline Frobenius is the pullback $phi = Phi^(*)$ of this $q$-power endomorphism: it is defined by absolute-Frobenius pullback, and the Witt Frobenius on $W \( bb(F)_q \) = bb(Z)_q$ is the identity because $q$ is prime #cite(label("StacksProject")) Tag 07N0. In degree-one cohomology pullback is additive in abelian homomorphisms. It reverses composition, but the displayed endomorphisms commute. Thus (5.9) gives the same operator polynomial with $phi$ and the Hecke pullbacks, not a reciprocal polynomial. This is the identity used in Proposition 28.

== 6 CM sources of the theta one-forms <sec:cm-sources>

We now prove Theorem 18, which identifies the rational CM sources of the theta classes. Section 5 constructed an algebraic Hecke action and a cubic relation with Frobenius. We calculate the active Hecke characters of the theta classes, insert them into that relation, and use degree-one Weil weight to isolate one Frobenius slope on every full character space. The Hecke projectors preserve the Hodge filtration, so these slopes determine the Hodge types of all scalar conjugates. Scalar descent will then give the required rational Hodge maps.

Retain the fields, moduli space, Albanese product, and Hecke operators of Section 5. In particular, the split good prime $q$ was chosen after fixing the finite theta inputs and their common principal level.

=== 6.1 The active spherical parameter <subsec:active-parameter>

We calculate the active Hecke characters in two steps. First we identify the geometric Hecke action on theta inputs as the inverse spherical action. We then compute that action on local inputs fixed by $"SL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)$, which form a cyclic module for a dilation operator. Rational scalar covariance will make its eigenvalues algebraic and encode the compact-place signs in their $q$-adic valuations. A lattice count then identifies the spherical parameter, with the raw Hecke normalization fixed.

Let $cal(H)_w^0$ be the rational subalgebra supported on positions with multiplier exponent zero, every nonactive selected block trivial, and active exponent sum zero. It is the spherical algebra of $"SL"_3 \( bb(Q)_q \)$.

#thm([Lemma 6.1.])[
  #emph[Every double coset in $cal(H)_w^0$ has all its branches within each component of $S$. On the original ball component, its pullback action on theta classes is the inverse spherical action on the active local Weil input: a local double coset acts through its inverse.]
] <lem:active-hecke-theta>

#proof[
  As a quasi-isogeny, such a position leaves the rational polarized period fixed. It changes only the active lattice and its opposite dual, keeping the marking and all other local data. This is also the description obtained by clearing the central shift in Lemma 21 and undoing its marking change. A determinant-exponent-zero lattice position has a representative in $"SL"_3 \( bb(Q)_q \)$. Adjusting determinant units in Smith bases shows that its double cosets and branches are exactly those for $"SL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \) without "SL"_3 \( bb(Q)_q \) \/ "SL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)$.
  For each component apply strong approximation to the special-unitary group of its rational Hermitian space. This group is simply connected and absolutely almost simple over $F$, and its factor at $mu$ is noncompact. Strong approximation away from all infinite places therefore makes its $F$-points dense in its finite adelic points #cite(label("MilneShimura2017")) Theorem 4.16. Put $K_q = "SL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)$. Write $Lambda^(\( j \))$ for the marked lattice of the component under consideration. For a branch with new active lattice $g^(- 1) Lambda_w^(\( j \))$, write $\( Lambda^(\( j \)) \)'$ for the full changed lattice and choose a rational representative $gamma$ whose active component lies in $K_q g$ and whose other finite components are integral and marking-preserving. Then $gamma \( Lambda^(\( j \)) \)' = Lambda^(\( j \))$, and $gamma$ identifies the target tuple with the transformed negative period line in the same component.
  On the original component, Lemmas 12 and 14 give
  $ gamma^(*) Theta #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] omega_f \( gamma \) phi.alt #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = Theta \( phi.alt \) . $
  Thus fixed-input pullback by the branch acts through $omega_f \( gamma \)^(- 1)$. Away from $w$ the chosen level fixes the input. At $w$ our good-prime choice makes it invariant under $K_q$; if $gamma_w = k g$, its inverse action on that input is therefore $omega_f \( g^(- 1) \)$. Summing the branches proves the inverse spherical formula. The sum is raw: no division by its degree and no additional power of $q$ occurs. ◻
]

We now compute this inverse spherical action on the local inputs.

At $w$, choose the Schrödinger position space to be the three-dimensional $w$-embedding summand, with lattice $bb(Z)_q^3$. By Lemma 14, the action of $"SL"_3 \( bb(Q)_q \)$ is inverse linear substitution. Every finite input is a finite sum of tensors separating this place from the other finite places. Our choice of $q$ makes the input invariant under $"SL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)$; averaging a separated decomposition over that compact group gives one whose local factors are invariant.

The invariant Schwartz functions are radial: $"SL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)$ is transitive on vectors with a fixed minimum coordinate valuation. Local constancy at zero and compact support therefore make this space the span of

$ b_j = upright(bold(1))_(q^j bb(Z)_q^3) \, #h(2em) j in bb(Z) . $

The functions $b_j$ are linearly independent. Define the normalized dilation

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ \( cal(D)_q b \) \( x \) = q^(3 \/ 2) b \( q^(- 1) x \) . $ <eq:radial-dilation>]

Then $cal(D)_q b_j = q^(3 \/ 2) b_(j + 1)$, so the radial space is a cyclic module for $bb(C) \[ cal(D)_q \, cal(D)_q^(- 1) \]$.

#thm([Lemma 6.2.])[
  #emph[Fix the input at all finite places other than $w$. There are $M > 0$, an element $r in E^times$, and a nonzero algebraic number $omega$ such that 
$ r macron(r) = 1 \, #h(2em) "val"_w \( r \) = M \, #h(2em) "val"_(macron(w)) \( r \) = - M \, $

 with zero valuation at every other finite prime, and the map from radial inputs to theta one-form classes factors through $cal(D)_q^M = omega$. In the covariance convention of Lemma 12, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ omega^2 = zeta product_(tau in Sigma) tau \( r \)^(- 3 d \( tau \)) $ <eq:radial-scalar-weight>]

 for a root of unity $zeta$. Every root of $X^M - omega$ has absolute value one under every complex embedding.]
] <lem:scalar-radial-relation>

#proof[
  Principalize a positive power $w^m = \( a \)$ and put $r = a \/ macron(a)$. It has norm one and the required valuations with $M = m$. At every other finite place it is a unit. Powers of an element of a compact unit group enter every prescribed open subgroup: its image in the finite quotient by that subgroup has finite order. Smoothness of the bare local Weil representation gives an open stabilizer of the fixed input in the metaplectic group; its image meets the scalar unit group in an open subgroup. Replacing $r$ by a common positive power therefore places it in the stabilizers needed at the finitely many exceptional places of the fixed input; replace $M$ accordingly.
  Multiplication by $r$ on $V ⊗_E P$ preserves its $F$-symplectic form because $r macron(r) = 1$. We use its #emph[bare rational symplectic] covariance, with paired metaplectic lifts, not the value of a chosen unitary splitting character. For a transformation $h$ of a local position space, the normalized linear action is
  $ \( rho \( h \) b \) \( x \) = \| det h \|_q^(- 1 \/ 2) b \( h^(- 1) x \) \, $
  up to the finite Weil phase of the bare lift. The linear-subgroup formula, rational summation invariance, and the double-cover control are in #cite(label("Weil1964")) I, no. 13; III, no. 41; IV, nos. 43–45. At $w$, the position action is the scalar $r_w$, of valuation $M$. Its unit part fixes a radial function, so the finite action there is $cal(D)_q^M$, up to a root of unity. At almost every other place the input is the unramified lattice vector and $r$ is integral. At the remaining places the chosen power fixes the input under a suitable bare lift; the paired lift differs only by its finite phase. Their product is again a root of unity. An arbitrary lift in the circle pushout would not justify this conclusion, which is why the bare rational action is used here.
  At infinity, multiplication by $r$ acts on the $tau$-embedding summand as the scalar $tau \( r \)$: this is the defining scalar action after tensoring the $E$-space through $tau$. On the $c tau$-summand it acts as $tau \( macron(r) \) = tau \( r \)^(- 1)$. It consequently fixes every chosen positive Lagrangian and the point of $D$. In the identity $g e_Z = e_(g Z) J$ of Lemma 12, $J$ is this action on the homology filtration. The squared scalar on the projected first tensor is the scalar on $det cal(F)_P$ times the square of the scalar on its projected $c$-summand. At $mu$, the filtration has scalar exponents $1 \, - 1 \, - 1$. Its determinant contributes $- 1$, and the squared projected conjugate factor contributes $- 2$. The total is $- 3$. At a compact pair, the selected full rank-three summand contributes $- 3 d \( tau \)$, with no projected tangent factor. Thus the squared infinite scalar is $product_(tau in Sigma) tau \( r \)^(- 3 d \( tau \))$. This calculation includes the scalar action on the determinant’s constant line, which is trivial only on $"SU" \( V \)$.
  The covariance formula has the finite transformed input on its left and the tensor action on its right. Since $r$ fixes the period, it gives $Theta \( cal(D)_q^M b \) = omega Theta \( b \)$, with the finite phase absorbed in $omega$, and its square is exactly (6.2). This equality of one-forms implies the asserted equality of cohomology classes.
  The right side of (6.2) is algebraic, so $omega$ is algebraic. Every complex embedding of $E$ sends $r$ to a number of modulus one, since $E$ is CM and $r macron(r) = 1$. Applying any scalar conjugation to the displayed identity shows that every complex conjugate of $omega$ has modulus one. The same is then true of each root of $X^M - omega$. ◻
]

For a finite separated sum of inputs, take the common stabilizers of all its other-place factors in this proof. The same $r \, M \, omega$ then works for that sum.

We use the half-modulus Satake convention, namely the parameter attached to normalized parabolic induction. We do not apply the additional central Hecke/Tate shift of Gross’s Section 8. In particular the trivial representation of $"GL"_2$ has parameter ${ q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) }$. On $"SL"_3$, a parameter is an unordered triple of nonzero scalars modulo a common scalar. We say that an active character occurs in a theta class when its simultaneous generalized eigenspace in the finite-dimensional Hecke span has a nonzero projection of that class.

#thm([Proposition 6.3.])[
  #emph[Every active spherical character occurring in a theta class of the multiplicity line $P$ has half-modulus projective parameter 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
 $ { q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \, e } \, $ <eq:active-satake-triple>]

 where $e = e_0^(- 1)$ for a root $e_0^M = omega$ from Lemma 24. It is algebraic and all its complex conjugates have modulus one. For every $sigma in "Gal" \( overline(bb(Q)) \/ bb(Q) \)$, 
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
 $ nu_iota \( sigma e \) = - 3 / 2 d #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( sigma \|_E \)^(- 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = - 3 / 2 v \( sigma \|_E \) . $ <eq:active-conjugate-valuations>]

 A scalar-conjugate active character has a representative of the form (6.3) with third entry $e' = plus.minus sigma e$.]
] <prop:theta-satake>

#proof[
  The active spherical action commutes with $cal(D)_q$. The radial Laurent module modulo $cal(D)_q^M - omega$ is a direct sum of one-dimensional spaces $cal(D)_q = e_0$, because $X^M - omega$ has distinct nonzero roots. It suffices to compute the spherical character on one of them. This decomposition concerns the radial quotient only; it asserts no semisimplicity for the ambient cohomological Hecke action.
  For this computation, extend the substitutions to
  $ rho \( g \) b \( x \) = \| det g \|_q^(- 1 \/ 2) b \( g^(- 1) x \) \, #h(2em) g in "GL"_3 \( bb(Q)_q \) \, $
  and put $K_3 = "GL"_3 \( bb(Z)_q \)$. This is a computational extension of the active $"SL"_3$-action, not a claim of theta equivariance for the full moduli similitude group. Let $sans(T)_i$ be the raw double coset of $"diag" \( q \, dots.h \, q \, 1 \, dots.h \, 1 \)$, with $i$ entries $q$, and $"vol" \( K_3 \) = 1$. Its right cosets correspond to sublattices between $q bb(Z)_q^3$ and $bb(Z)_q^3$ of index $q^i$. For $i = 1 \, 2$ there are $q^2 + q + 1$ such lattices. The numbers containing a fixed primitive vector are respectively $q + 1$ and $1$. Since $rho \( g \) b_0$ is $q^(i \/ 2)$ times the characteristic function of its sublattice, we obtain
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
   $ sans(T)_1 b_0 & = q^(1 \/ 2) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( q + 1 \) b_0 + q^2 b_1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
sans(T)_2 b_0 & = q #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] b_0 + \( q^2 + q \) b_1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
sans(T)_3 b_0 & = cal(D)_q b_0 . $ <eq:radial-minuscule-actions>]
  For example, vectors in $q bb(Z)_q^3$ belong to every sublattice, while a primitive vector is tested by its nonzero class in $bb(F)_q^3$; the displayed counts are the numbers of the corresponding planes or lines containing that class.
  On the quotient $cal(D)_q = e_0$ we have $b_1 = q^(- 3 \/ 2) e_0 b_0$. Substitution into (6.5) gives the values
  $ q thin e_1 \( e_0 \, q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \) \, #h(2em) q thin e_2 \( e_0 \, q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \) \, #h(2em) e_3 \( e_0 \, q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \) \, $
  where $e_i$ is the $i$-th elementary symmetric polynomial. In the half-modulus Satake isomorphism the raw minuscule cosets have transforms $q e_1 \, q e_2 \, e_3$; these generate the spherical algebra, with $e_3$ invertible. See #cite(label("Gross1998")) Equation (3.14) and §6, where the $"GL"_n$ formula is attributed to Tamagawa. The character just computed therefore has parameter ${ e_0 \, q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) }$.
  The geometric action on theta inputs is inverse by Lemma 23. Inversion sends the half-modulus parameter to its reciprocal, without a degree factor. One can check this on the same generators: $sans(T)_1^or = sans(T)_3^(- 1) sans(T)_2$, $sans(T)_2^or = sans(T)_3^(- 1) sans(T)_1$, and $sans(T)_3^or = sans(T)_3^(- 1)$, where $f^or \( g \) = f \( g^(- 1) \)$. Their transforms are the corresponding elementary symmetric functions of the reciprocal triple. After interchanging the first two entries, the active projective parameter is therefore ${ q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \, e_0^(- 1) }$.
  The determinant-zero $"GL"_3$ and $"SL"_3$ double cosets agree as in Lemma 23. Their Satake unipotent integrals and modulus factors agree as well. The half-sum of positive roots is $\( 1 \, 0 \, - 1 \)$, integral on the relevant cocharacter lattices; the Satake comparison is consequently defined over rational coefficients #cite(label("Gross1998")) Proposition 3.6. Scalar conjugation of an active numerical character therefore conjugates its projective parameter. It may change the two entries $q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2)$ by their common sign. Removing that sign projectively leaves third entry $plus.minus sigma e$.
  It remains to calculate the valuation. Put $g = sigma \|_E$. By the definition of the prime labels,
  $ nu_iota \( sigma tau \( r \) \) = "val"_(frak(p)_(g tau)) \( r \) . $
  This is nonzero only when $g tau = 1$ or $c$. Among the representatives $Sigma$, the contributing one is $g^(- 1)$ with valuation $- M$, or $c g^(- 1)$ with valuation $M$. Here $c$ is central in $G_E$, and oddness of $d$ gives in either case
  $ sum_(tau in Sigma) d \( tau \) nu_iota \( sigma tau \( r \) \) = - M d \( g^(- 1) \) . $
  Taking valuations in (6.2) and in $e_0^M = omega$ yields $nu_iota \( sigma e_0 \) = 3 / 2 d \( g^(- 1) \)$. Since $e = e_0^(- 1)$ and $d \( g^(- 1) \) = v \( g \)$, this proves (6.4). The unit-modulus assertion follows from Lemma 24. ◻
]

The active character is now known, including the valuation of every scalar conjugate. The full Hecke character may still multiply its three Satake entries by a common scalar. The following finite-order relations will control that scalar when we apply the Frobenius polynomial of Proposition 22.

#thm([Lemma 6.4.])[
  #emph[The central operators $T_3$ and $C_0$ have finite order on the full tuple space $S$ and on $A_S$.]
] <lem:central-finite-order>

#proof[
  Each central position has a unique lattice choice. For $T_3$, use the norm-one element $r$ constructed in Lemma 24; only its valuations and norm are needed here. The scalar $r$ takes the $T_3^M$-target lattice back to the source lattice on the active and opposite blocks. It preserves every polarized period because it is an $E$-scalar of norm one. Its denominators are supported at $q$, so a further power is one on the $N$-marking. That power of $T_3$ is the identity on every tuple.
  For $C_0$, let $I$ be the product of the primes where its full exponent is one. There is exactly one from each conjugate pair, including $macron(w)$, and $I macron(I) = \( q \)$. Choose $I^m = \( a \)$. Then
  $ a macron(a) = q^m u $
  for a totally positive unit $u in cal(O)_F^times$. The scalar $b = a^2 \/ u$ has exactly the valuations of $I^(2 m)$ and satisfies
  $ b macron(b) = q^(2 m) . $
  It identifies the corresponding powered target lattice with the source, with exactly the required polarization multiplier. A further power fixes the marking. This argument works on every rational Hermitian component because an $E$-scalar preserves each of its embedding filtrations. Thus a power of $C_0$ is also the identity on the full tuple space. The induced Albanese homomorphisms have finite order as well. ◻
]

=== 6.2 From Frobenius slopes to Hodge types <subsec:slopes-to-types>

We now combine the Frobenius polynomial with the theta parameters to determine the Hodge types of the relevant Hecke spaces. The polynomial will force a single Newton slope on each full generalized character space. To turn this into a Hodge type, we also need its projector to preserve the Hodge filtration. We first construct these projectors and explain the filtered argument.

Let $bb(T)$ be the image of the full spherical Hecke algebra in $"End"_(bb(Q)) H^1 \( A_S \, bb(Q) \)$. It is a finite-dimensional commutative $bb(Q)$-algebra; its active subalgebra is the image of $cal(H)_w^0$. A numerical character is a $bb(Q)$-algebra homomorphism $chi : bb(T) arrow.r overline(bb(Q))$. Inside a character value, the symbol of an endomorphism denotes its degree-one pullback. After a finite extension of algebraic coefficients, $bb(T)$ is a product of local Artinian algebras. Its product idempotents $p_chi$ give the simultaneous generalized character spaces

$ H_chi = p_chi H^1 \( A_S \, overline(bb(Q)) \) . $

If $eta$ is a character of the active subalgebra, its generalized space is

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
 $ H_eta^(upright("act")) = xor.big_(chi \|_(upright("act")) = eta) H_chi . $ <eq:active-generalized-space>]

Indeed the image of the active idempotent in a local factor of the full algebra is either zero or one, as decided by its residue character. Thus the sum includes every full extension of $eta$ and all of its nilpotent parts. No semisimplicity of the Hecke operators is assumed.

Each Hecke operator is induced by a rational endomorphism of $A_S$ defined over $K$. In degree-one Betti, de Rham, and crystalline cohomology, pullback is additive in abelian homomorphisms: in the Künneth decomposition of $H^1 \( A times A \)$, pullback by addition has both components equal to the identity, as restriction to the two axes shows. Pullback reverses composition, but the Hecke endomorphisms commute. Pullback on Betti $H^1$ is faithful by Lemma 3, so polynomial relations in $bb(T)$ are also relations among the corresponding endomorphisms. The same degree-one functoriality preserves those relations in de Rham and crystalline cohomology. Thus the $p_chi$ remain idempotents in these degree-one realizations after extension of coefficients. They need not have rational coefficients; what matters is that they are algebraic-coefficient polynomials in extending endomorphisms.

The following lemma applies to precisely these projected summands. Its use of both complementary projectors is what turns weak admissibility into equality of the Hodge and Newton numbers on each summand.

#thm([Lemma 6.5.])[
  #emph[Let $cal(A) \/ bb(Z)_q$ be an abelian scheme with generic fiber $A$, and put 
$ D_A = H_(upright("dR"))^1 \( A \/ bb(Q)_q \) . $

 Equip $D_A$ with its Hodge filtration and its good-reduction crystalline Frobenius $phi_A$. Then $D_A$ is weakly admissible, its filtration has only indices $0$ and $1$, and endomorphisms extending over $bb(Z)_q$ preserve the filtration and commute with $phi_A$.]
  #emph[Let $L \/ bb(Q)_q$ be finite. Suppose that an $L$-linear idempotent $p$ on $D_A ⊗_(bb(Q)_q) L$ preserves the filtration and commutes with $phi_A ⊗ 1$. If every Frobenius slope on its image is $t in { 0 \, 1 }$, then the induced filtration satisfies 
$ F^1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p \( D_A ⊗ L \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = cases(delim: "{", 0 \, & t = 0 \,, p \( D_A ⊗ L \) \, & t = 1 .) $

]
] <lem:filtered-hecke-summands>

#proof[
  Good-reduction crystalline comparison identifies
  $ D_(upright("cris")) #h(-0.16667em) (H_(upright(acute(e) t))^1 \( A_(overline(bb(Q))_q) \, bb(Q)_q \)) tilde.eq H_(upright("cris"))^1 \( cal(A)_(bb(F)_q) \/ bb(Z)_q \) \[ 1 \/ q \] $
  and identifies the filtered de Rham realization with $D_A$. These identifications are functorial for extending endomorphisms and compatible with Frobenius and filtration #cite(label("BhattMorrowScholze2018")) Theorem 1.1(i), Theorem 12.1, and §12.2.#footnote[For the proper formal model and the algebraic/analytic cohomology identifications, see #cite(label("Bosch2014FormalRigid")) §7.2, p. 161 and #cite(label("Scholze2013RigidAnalyticHodge")) Theorems 9.1 and 9.3, read with #cite(label("Scholze2016RigidAnalyticHodgeCorrigendum")). Passage from the algebraic closure to its completion uses #cite(label("StacksProject")) Tag 0DDG. The étale comparisons are applied with $bb(Z) \/ q^n$-coefficients, then passed to inverse limits and rationalized.] As in Subsection 5.4, crystalline Frobenius is the pullback of the special fiber’s $q$-power endomorphism: Witt Frobenius on $W \( bb(F)_q \) = bb(Z)_q$ is the identity #cite(label("StacksProject")) Tags 07N3 and 07N5. Crystalline representations give weakly admissible filtered modules #cite(label("BrinonConrad2009")) Theorem 9.3.4. The degree-one Hodge filtration has only indices $0$ and $1$, and functoriality gives the assertion about extending endomorphisms.
  The extension by $L$ changes coefficients, not the geometric base field. With Frobenius acting trivially on this coefficient factor, the underlying $bb(Q)_q$-filtered Frobenius module
  $ #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] D_A ⊗_(bb(Q)_q) L \, med phi_A ⊗ 1 \, med F^i D_A ⊗_(bb(Q)_q) L #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] $
  is a direct sum of $\[ L : bb(Q)_q \]$ copies of $D_A$, and is weakly admissible. Since $p$ and $1 - p$ both preserve the filtration and commute with Frobenius, their images split it as filtered Frobenius modules. Weak admissibility gives $t_H lt.eq t_N$ on each image. The Hodge and Newton numbers are additive, and are equal for the whole module. They are therefore equal on each image separately.
  Let $m = dim_(bb(Q)_q) p \( D_A ⊗ L \)$. A pure slope $t$ gives $t_N = t m$ after restriction of scalars: all $bb(Q)_q$-conjugates of a $q$-adic algebraic number have the same valuation, including when $L \/ bb(Q)_q$ is ramified. On the other hand,
  $ t_H = dim_(bb(Q)_q) F^1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] p \( D_A ⊗ L \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, $
  because the only filtration indices are $0$ and $1$. Equality of these two numbers proves the assertion. The general coefficient formalism is also described in #cite(label("Hellmann2011")) Proposition 2.17(ii) and Corollary 2.22. ◻
]

The requirement on the idempotent is essential. For example, $phi = "diag" \( 1 \, q \)$ on $bb(Q)_q e_0 xor bb(Q)_q e_1$, with only filtration indices $0 \, 1$ and $F^1 = chevron.l e_0 + e_1 chevron.r$, is weakly admissible, but its slope-one line has induced $F^1 = 0$. Its slope projector does not preserve the filtration. The Hecke projectors do preserve it, because they are formed from extending algebraic endomorphisms.

We can therefore obtain a pure Hodge type by proving that a full generalized Hecke space has slope zero or one. The finite-order central relation controls the common scalar in a full extension of the active projective parameter, and the Frobenius polynomial then determines the slope.

Fix an active character $eta$ with projective parameter

$ { q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \, e' } \, $

where $e' = e$ is supplied by Proposition 25, or $e' = plus.minus sigma e$ is the parameter of a scalar-conjugate character. Thus $e'$ is algebraic, all its complex conjugates have modulus one, and $nu_iota \( e' \) in { - 3 \/ 2 \, 3 \/ 2 }$. For a full character $chi$ extending $eta$, its active $"GL"_3$ parameter is a scalar multiple of this triple. Absorb the numerical value of $C_0$ into that scalar and write the resulting triple as

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
 $ \( alpha_1 \, alpha_2 \, alpha_3 \) = b \( q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \, e' \) . $ <eq:full-satake-triple>]

The determinant coordinate is the character value of $D_3 = C_0^3 T_3$. Lemma 26 therefore gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
 $ b^3 e' upright(" is a root of unity") . $ <eq:satake-central-relation>]

In particular $b$ is algebraic and every complex conjugate of $b$ has modulus one.

Write $e_i$ for the elementary symmetric polynomials in three variables. The raw minuscule double cosets, with the integral subgroup of volume one, have Satake transforms $q e_1 \, med q e_2 \, med e_3$; this is #cite(label("Gross1998")) Equation (3.14) and §6 for $"GL"_3$. Since $D_i = C_0^i T_i$, absorption of the $C_0$-value into $b$ gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
 $ chi \( D_1 \) = q e_1 \( alpha \) \, #h(2em) chi \( D_2 \) = q e_2 \( alpha \) \, #h(2em) chi \( D_3 \) = e_3 \( alpha \) . $ <eq:raw-D-character>]

These are numerical character values. On $H_chi$ the operators may still have nilpotent parts.

#thm([Proposition 6.6.])[
  #emph[Let $eta$ be an active numerical character with projective parameter ${ q^(1 \/ 2) \, q^(- 1 \/ 2) \, e' }$ supplied by Proposition 25 or by scalar conjugation, and put 
$ t_eta = 1 / 2 - 1 / 3 nu_iota \( e' \) in { 0 \, 1 } . $

 For every full numerical character $chi$ extending $eta$, the space $H_chi ⊗_(overline(bb(Q))) bb(C)$ is contained in $H^(1 \, 0) \( A_S \)$ if $t_eta = 1$, and in $H^(0 \, 1) \( A_S \)$ if $t_eta = 0$. Consequently $H_eta^(upright("act")) ⊗_(overline(bb(Q))) bb(C)$ has the same pure Hodge type.]
] <prop:active-hodge-types>

#proof[
  Fix a full character $chi$ extending $eta$; if there is none, the assertion is vacuous. We first use Weil weight to select the Frobenius root. We then apply Lemma 27 and compare the resulting filtration ranks with those on the complex Betti realization.
  At the fixed completion $K_v = bb(Q)_q$, write
  $ D_S = H_(upright("dR"))^1 \( \( A_S \)_(K_v) \/ bb(Q)_q \) $
  and let $phi$ be its crystalline Frobenius. If $Phi$ is the $q$-power Frobenius endomorphism of $tilde(A)_S \/ bb(F)_q$ used in Proposition 22, then $phi = Phi^(*)$ under crystalline comparison. The Hecke endomorphisms extend over $bb(Z)_q$; their reductions commute with $Phi$, so their pullbacks commute with $phi$.
  By additivity of pullback on abelian degree-one cohomology, the integral identity of Proposition 22 gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
   $ phi^3 - D_1^(*) phi^2 + q D_2^(*) phi - q^3 D_3^(*) = 0 . $ <eq:cohomological-frobenius-polynomial>]
  Pullback reverses composition, but all operators in this formula commute.
  Choose a prime $ell eq.not q$ and put $V_ell tilde(A)_S = T_ell tilde(A)_S ⊗_(bb(Z)_ell) bb(Q)_ell$. Weil’s theorem for abelian varieties over $bb(F)_q$ gives
  $ P_Phi \( X \) = det #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] X - Phi divides V_ell tilde(A)_S #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] in bb(Z) \[ X \] \, $
  all of whose complex roots have absolute value $q^(1 \/ 2)$. The prime-to-$q$ Tate-module action on endomorphisms is faithful. Cayley–Hamilton therefore gives $P_Phi \( Phi \) = 0$ in the endomorphism ring, and degree-one additivity gives $P_Phi \( phi \) = 0$. These statements hold for an arbitrary abelian variety over $bb(F)_q$; see #cite(label("Milne1986AbelianVarieties")) Lemma 12.2, Proposition 12.9, and Theorem 19.1(c) with its proof. No CM or ordinary hypothesis on $tilde(A)_S$ is involved. This uses the $q$-power endomorphism and its pullback, not a reciprocal Galois-Frobenius convention.
  Transport the algebraic coefficients of $p_chi$ and $chi$ by $iota$. On the corresponding generalized summand of $D_S ⊗_(bb(Q)_q) overline(bb(Q))_q$, the commuting Hecke maps and $phi$ can be simultaneously triangularized. Equations (6.10) and (6.9) imply that every diagonal Frobenius eigenvalue is a root of
  $ X^3 - q e_1 \( alpha \) X^2 + q^2 e_2 \( alpha \) X - q^3 e_3 \( alpha \) = product_(j = 1)^3 \( X - q alpha_j \) . $
  Thus the candidates, before applying $iota$, are
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
   $ b q^(3 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) b q^(1 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) q b e' . $ <eq:frobenius-candidates>]
  Each is algebraic. If its image under $iota$ is a root of the rational polynomial $P_Phi$, the algebraic number itself is a root of $P_Phi$. We may therefore apply the complex absolute-value condition. The three absolute values are respectively
  $ q^(3 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) q^(1 \/ 2) \, #h(2em) q \, $
  because every complex conjugate of $b$ and $e'$ has modulus one. Only $b q^(1 \/ 2)$ remains.
  The weight condition has now selected one Newton slope on the entire generalized summand. Equation (6.8) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
   $ nu_iota \( b q^(1 \/ 2) \) = 1 / 2 - 1 / 3 nu_iota \( e' \) = t_eta . $ <eq:active-hodge-slope>]
  This conclusion concerns every Frobenius eigenvalue; Jordan blocks do not change it. Choose a finite extension $L \/ bb(Q)_q$ containing the coefficients of the transported idempotent and numerical character. The idempotent $p_chi$ is a polynomial in extending Hecke maps, so it preserves the Hodge filtration and commutes with $phi$. Lemma 27 makes its filtration pure of index $t_eta$.
  It remains to relate this filtered rank to the Hodge type of the same complex generalized space. Put
  $ V_(upright("dR")) = H_(upright("dR"))^1 \( A_S \/ K \) . $
  Its filtration and Hecke operators are defined over the fixed field $K$. For a numerical character $chi$, choose a polynomial expression for $p_chi$, and let $k_chi subset overline(bb(Q))$ be the actual compositum of $K$ and its algebraic coefficients. Evaluating that polynomial in the fixed operators gives an idempotent on $V_(upright("dR")) ⊗_K k_chi$. Its induced filtration is its projected filtration, since the idempotent preserves $F^bullet$. The dimensions of its image and of this filtration are ranks of matrices over $k_chi$; they are unchanged under the restriction of the fixed embedding $iota$ to $k_chi$.
  Over the given embedding $K subset bb(C)$, Betti–de Rham comparison intertwines the same operators and polynomial. It identifies these ranks with the filtration ranks on $H_chi ⊗_(overline(bb(Q))) bb(C)$. For a scalar-conjugate numerical character $sigma chi$, repeat this argument with $p_(sigma chi)$ in the same $K$-defined operators. On Betti cohomology their rational matrices give $sigma H_chi = H_(sigma chi)$. We have therefore computed the type of the scalar-conjugated Betti space on the same complex variety. Each conjugate projector is evaluated separately in the fixed $K$-defined operators. This works even when $K$ intersects the character field, since it uses ranks over their actual compositum.
  Finally the complex Hecke operators preserve both $H^(1 \, 0)$ and $H^(0 \, 1)$. Hence a full projected $F^1$ means that the whole generalized space is holomorphic, and zero projected $F^1$ means that it is antiholomorphic. Equation (6.12) depends only on $eta$, so summing all full extensions gives the assertion for $H_eta^(upright("act"))$. ◻
]

=== 6.3 Rational CM sources for the theta classes <subsec:rational-cm-sources>

The preceding proposition determines the type of every vector in each scalar-conjugate active Hecke space. We can therefore apply scalar descent to maps with arbitrary algebraic Betti vectors in these spaces. This will prove the rational-map span asserted in Theorem 18.

#proof[
  Use the Albanese identification $H^1 \( A_S \, bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H^1 \( S \, bb(Q) \)$. Extend a theta class from $S_0$ by zero on the other components of $S$. This is compatible with the active algebra, because Lemma 23 makes that algebra component-preserving. The class is holomorphic by Proposition 15. Decompose it into the active generalized character spaces of Equation (6.6). For each nonzero component, let $eta$ be its active character and $e$ its parameter from Proposition 25. For every $sigma in "Gal" \( overline(bb(Q)) \/ bb(Q) \)$, put $g = sigma \|_E$. That proposition and Proposition 28 give
