  For a finite collection of inputs, all level choices are made before the auxiliary prime is chosen, and only finitely many primes are excluded. The preceding argument therefore applies to that collection at one sufficiently deep level. If $pi : S'_0 arrow.r S_0$ is a deeper principal cover, then $pi^(*)$ is a rational Hodge map and the theta classes pull back to their classes on $S'_0$. Applying $pi^(*)$ to Equation (5.1) proves persistence. ◻
]

By Lemma 3, each rational map in Equation (5.1) is, up to a rational multiple, pullback along a morphism $S_0 arrow.r B \( v \)$.

