#proof[
  The four-factor switch theorem is now proved for every Galois CM field. Proposition 7 consequently places every rational Hodge class on a CM abelian variety in its rational cycle span. Its final scalar descent takes place on the original variety. Finite products and powers are CM, so the same conclusion covers them. ◻
]

== 8 Generalized Hodge and arithmetic consequences <cc:section>

Theorem 1 applies to products of CM abelian varieties and therefore algebraizes rational Hodge correspondences between them. We use this to obtain the algebraic supports predicted by the generalized Hodge conjecture, following Abdulali’s effective-realization argument #cite(label("Abdulali2005CM")) Lemma 1 and Theorems 3–4. Hazama had earlier reduced this implication to codimension-two Hodge classes on a special family of CM abelian varieties #cite(label("Hazama2003CM")) Theorem 8.3. We then apply Milne’s Hodge-to-Tate and polarization theorems to obtain the Tate and Hodge standard conjectures for abelian varieties. A final application uses CM transport to algebraize specialized Hodge classes on arbitrary good-reduction abelian varieties.

=== 8.1 The generalized Hodge conjecture <the-generalized-hodge-conjecture>

A pure rational Hodge structure is #emph[effective] if all its nonzero Hodge components have nonnegative indices. Recall that $V \( r \)^(p \, q) = V^(p + r \, q + r)$, so the twist $V \( r \)$ has weight $"wt" \( V \) - 2 r$. The Hodge circle of a pure Hodge structure $V$ acts on $V^(p \, q)$ by $z^(p - q)$, for $\| z \| = 1$. Let $J \( V \)$ be the smallest algebraic subgroup of $"GL" \( V \)$ over $bb(Q)$ whose real points contain that circle. A polarizable $V$ is #emph[of CM type] if $J \( V \)$ is a torus, with the trivial group allowed. Every Hodge substructure of the cohomology of a CM abelian variety is of CM type: its Hodge-circle group is an image of the torus acting on degree-one cohomology.

For a smooth projective complex variety $X$, the geometric coniveau filtration is

$ N^r H^k \( X \, bb(Q) \) = sum_(Z subset X upright(" closed")\
"codim"_X Z gt.eq r) ker #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H^k \( X \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r H^k \( X \\ Z \, bb(Q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

Grothendieck’s corrected generalized Hodge conjecture says that a rational Hodge substructure $W subset H^k \( X \, bb(Q) \)$ lies in $N^r H^k \( X \, bb(Q) \)$ whenever $W \( r \)$ is effective; see #cite(label("Grothendieck1969General")) §2 and #cite(label("Hazama2003CM")) §2. Equivalently, one closed subset of codimension at least $r$ supports all of $W$. Indeed, finite dimensionality permits taking a finite union of the supports needed for a basis of $W$.

We will obtain such a support by algebraizing a Hodge map from an auxiliary CM abelian variety. To get the required codimension, that variety must realize an effective twist of $W$ in cohomology of the same weight; the twisting index will determine the codimension of the resulting correspondence. The next lemma constructs it by expressing the Hodge indices at each embedding as sums of weight-one indices.

#thm([Lemma 8.1.])[
  (Effective realization of a simple CM Hodge structure).  #emph[Let $W$ be a nonzero simple polarizable rational Hodge structure of CM type and weight $k$. Put 
$ r_0 = min { p : W^(p \, k - p) eq.not 0 } \, #h(2em) n = k - 2 r_0 . $

 Then $n gt.eq 0$, and there is a CM abelian variety $B$, possibly a point, and a surjective rational Hodge map 
$ H^n \( B \, bb(Q) \) \( - r_0 \) arrow.r W . $

]
] <cc:realization>

#proof[
  Set $U = W \( r_0 \)$. Hodge symmetry shows that $U$ is effective of weight $n gt.eq 0$, with smallest Hodge index zero. If $n = 0$, every type of $U$ is $\( 0 \, 0 \)$. Simplicity then gives $U tilde.eq bb(Q)$, and we take $B$ to be a point.
  Suppose $n > 0$. We first show that $U$ is a line over a CM field; we will then decompose its Hodge type into weight-one CM types. Write $T = J \( U \)$. Let $E_0$ be the $bb(Q)$-subalgebra of $"End"_(bb(Q)) \( U \)$ generated by the image of $T$. After splitting the torus, its action is diagonal by characters, so $E_0$ is a commutative finite étale algebra. Its field idempotents and every subspace linear over one field factor are $T$-stable. Simplicity of $U$ therefore forces $E_0$ to be a field $E$ and $dim_E U = 1$.
  We check that $E$ is a CM field. Let the Weil operator $C_U$ act as $i^(p - q)$ on $U^(p \, q)$. By polarizability there is a rational Hodge pairing $psi$, with the conventional weight-dependent sign absorbed in its definition, for which
  $ q \( x \, y \) = psi \( C_U x \, y \) $
  is positive definite on $U_(bb(R))$. The adjoint $dagger$ for $psi$ sends $t in T$ to $t^(- 1)$, hence preserves $E$. Every element of $E$ commutes with $C_U$, so $dagger$ is also the adjoint for $q$. On the real finite étale algebra $E_(bb(R))$, it must fix each primitive idempotent: if an idempotent $e$ were sent to a different one, then $e^dagger e = 0$, contrary to $q \( e x \, e x \) = q \( x \, e^dagger e x \) > 0$ for some $x$. On a complex factor it must act by complex conjugation, since the identity would give $i^dagger i = - 1$, again contrary to positivity. Thus if $dagger$ is trivial on $E$, the field is totally real. If it is nontrivial, a real embedding would force $dagger$ to be trivial on $E$, so there are no real embeddings; the fixed algebra in $E_(bb(R))$ is then a product of copies of $bb(R)$. Its fixed field is therefore totally real, and $E$ is CM.
  The totally real case cannot occur here. The Hodge circle acts through $E_(bb(R))^times$; its image in each real scalar factor is a compact connected subgroup of $bb(R)^times$, hence is trivial. All Hodge types of $U$ would then have equal indices. Since their smallest index is zero, this would force $n = 0$. Consequently $E$ is CM, and $dagger$ is its complex conjugation.
  Let $Sigma = "Hom" \( E \, bb(C) \)$, and write $macron(sigma)$ for the conjugate of $sigma$. Since $E$ acts by Hodge endomorphisms and $U$ is an $E$-line,
  $ U_(bb(C)) = xor.big_(sigma in Sigma) U_sigma \, #h(2em) U_sigma upright(" has type ") \( a_sigma \, n - a_sigma \) \, $
  where $0 lt.eq a_sigma lt.eq n$ and $a_sigma + a_(macron(sigma)) = n$. Choose $n$ CM types $Phi_1 \, dots.h \, Phi_n$ of $E$ with
  $ \# { j : sigma in Phi_j } = a_sigma . $
  This is possible independently for each conjugate pair: put $sigma$ into any $a_sigma$ lists and $macron(sigma)$ into the remaining lists.
  For each $j$, give the rational vector space $H_j = E$ the weight-one Hodge structure whose $sigma$-line has type $\( 1 \, 0 \)$ exactly when $sigma in Phi_j$. The polarization and Riemann construction of Section 2, surrounding Equation (2.2), applies to any CM field, with no Galois hypothesis. It gives a CM abelian variety $B_j$ with $H^1 \( B_j \, bb(Q) \) tilde.eq H_j$.
  Identify $U$ and the $H_j$ with $E$ as $E$-lines. The rational multiplication map
  $ E^(⊗_(bb(Q)) n) arrow.r E \, #h(2em) x_1 ⊗ dots.h.c ⊗ x_n mapsto x_1 dots.h.c x_n $
  is surjective. Over $bb(C)$ it kills tensors with unequal embedding labels, while the tensor with all labels equal to $sigma$ has type $\( a_sigma \, n - a_sigma \)$ and maps to $U_sigma$. It is therefore a Hodge map to $U$. For $B = product_(j = 1)^n B_j$, its source is the rational Künneth summand $⨂_j H^1 \( B_j \, bb(Q) \)$ of $H^n \( B \, bb(Q) \)$. Extending by zero on the other summands and twisting by $\( - r_0 \)$ proves the lemma. ◻
]

#thm([Corollary 8.2.])[
  (Generalized Hodge conjecture for CM abelian varieties).  #emph[Let $A$ be a complex CM abelian variety. For integers $k \, r gt.eq 0$ and a rational Hodge substructure $W subset H^k \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ such that $W \( r \)$ is effective, there is a closed algebraic subset $Z subset A$ of codimension at least $r$ such that 
$ W subset ker #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] H^k \( A \, bb(Q) \) arrow.r H^k \( A \\ Z \, bb(Q) \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $

 This includes every finite product of CM abelian varieties and every power of such a product.]
] <cc:ghc>

#proof[
  The zero substructure permits $Z = diameter$. Otherwise, semisimplicity of polarizable rational Hodge structures reduces the claim to a simple summand $W$, after which we take the finite union of the resulting supports. Let $r_0$ be its smallest Hodge index. Then $r_0 gt.eq r$. Also $r_0 lt.eq k \/ 2 lt.eq dim A$, since $W eq.not 0$. Put $n = k - 2 r_0$, take $B$ from Lemma 30, and write $b = dim B$. Composing the map of that lemma with $W arrow.r.hook H^k \( A \, bb(Q) \)$ gives
  $ f : H^n \( B \, bb(Q) \) \( - r_0 \) arrow.r H^k \( A \, bb(Q) \) \, #h(2em) "im" \( f \) = W . $
  By Poincaré duality, $f$ corresponds to a rational Hodge class of type $\( b + r_0 \, b + r_0 \)$ in
  $ H^(2 b - n) \( B \, bb(Q) \) ⊗ H^k \( A \, bb(Q) \) subset H^(2 b + 2 r_0) \( B times A \, bb(Q) \) . $
  The product $B times A$ is CM. Theorem 1 therefore represents this exact class by $Gamma in "CH"^(b + r_0) \( B times A \)_(bb(Q))$, whose action in degree $n$ is $f$.
  Choose a finite rational cycle representing $Gamma$, and let $Z$ be the projection of its support to $A$. Every component of that cycle has dimension
  $ dim \( B times A \) - \( b + r_0 \) = dim A - r_0 . $
  The projection is proper, so $Z$ is closed and $"codim"_A Z gt.eq r_0 gt.eq r$. The correspondence restricts to zero on $B times \( A \\ Z \)$; functoriality of its cohomological action therefore makes its image $W$ restrict to zero on $A \\ Z$. ◻
]

=== 8.2 The Tate conjecture over finite fields <the-tate-conjecture-over-finite-fields>

Let $A$ be an abelian variety over $bb(F)_q$, put $macron(A) = A times_(bb(F)_q) overline(bb(F)_q)$, and write $G_q = "Gal" \( overline(bb(F)_q) \/ bb(F)_q \)$. For a prime $ell eq.not "char" \( bb(F)_q \)$, the rational Tate conjecture in codimension $r$ asks whether cycles over $bb(F)_q$ span $H_(upright(acute(e) t))^(2 r) \( macron(A) \, bb(Q)_ell \( r \) \)^(G_q)$.

#thm([Corollary 8.3.])[
  (Tate conjecture for abelian varieties over finite fields).  #emph[For every abelian variety $A \/ bb(F)_q$, every integer $r gt.eq 0$, and every prime $ell eq.not "char" \( bb(F)_q \)$, the cycle-class map 
$ "CH"^r \( A \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q)_ell arrow.r H_(upright(acute(e) t))^(2 r) \( macron(A) \, bb(Q)_ell \( r \) \)^(G_q) $

 is surjective. Numerical equivalence and $ell$-adic homological equivalence coincide on rational algebraic cycles on $macron(A)$.]
] <cc:tate>

#proof[
  Apply Milne’s Theorem 7.1 and his formulation (0.1) #cite(label("Milne1999Tate")), with the correction in #cite(label("cc:Milne1999Addenda")): the cofinal auxiliary Galois CM fields are enlarged to contain a real subfield of degree $> 2$ inert at the residue prime. The theorem’s hypothesis is supplied by Theorem 1. It gives the stated equality of equivalence relations and algebraic spanning of the geometric Tate classes: those fixed over some finite field of definition.
  Let $alpha$ lie in the displayed $G_q$-invariant space. The geometric conclusion writes $alpha$ as a finite $bb(Q)_ell$-linear combination of cycle classes. All the cycles in this expression descend to $A_(bb(F)_(q^m))$ for one $m$; let $z_m in "CH"^r \( A_(bb(F)_(q^m)) \) ⊗_(bb(Z)) bb(Q)_ell$ denote the resulting combination. For the finite projection $pi_m : A_(bb(F)_(q^m)) arrow.r A$, proper pushforward and base change for cycle classes give
  $ "cl"_ell #h(-0.16667em) (1 / m \( pi_m \)_(*) z_m) = 1 / m sum_(sigma in "Gal" \( bb(F)_(q^m) \/ bb(F)_q \)) sigma \( alpha \) = alpha . $
  Thus $m^(- 1) \( pi_m \)_(*) z_m$ is the required class over $bb(F)_q$. Division by $m$ is valid in $bb(Q)_ell$, even when $m$ is divisible by $ell$ or by the characteristic. ◻
]

Milne’s implication compares CM and finite-field abelian motives. It does not assert that each Tate class lifts to a Hodge class on a single CM lifting, even up to isogeny #cite(label("Milne1999Tate")) Introduction, Remark (a).

=== 8.3 The Hodge standard conjecture <the-hodge-standard-conjecture>

Let $A$ be an abelian variety of dimension $g$ over an algebraically closed field $K$, and let $A_(upright("num"))^r \( A \)_(bb(Q))$ be the space of rational codimension-$r$ cycles modulo numerical equivalence. A #emph[rational ample divisor class] is an $h in A_(upright("num"))^1 \( A \)_(bb(Q))$ for which some positive integral multiple is the numerical class of an ample Cartier divisor. For integers $0 lt.eq r lt.eq g \/ 2$, put

$ P_h^r \( A \) = ker #h(-0.16667em) (h^(g - 2 r + 1) : A_(upright("num"))^r \( A \)_(bb(Q)) arrow.r A_(upright("num"))^(g - r + 1) \( A \)_(bb(Q))) . $

#thm([Corollary 8.4.])[
  (Hodge standard conjecture for abelian varieties).  #emph[Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field and $A \/ K$ an abelian variety of dimension $g$. For every prime $ell eq.not "char" \( K \)$, numerical and $ell$-adic homological equivalence coincide on rational algebraic cycles on $A$. For every rational ample divisor class $h$ and every integer $0 lt.eq r lt.eq g \/ 2$, the rational symmetric form 
$ P_h^r \( A \) times P_h^r \( A \) arrow.r bb(Q) \, #h(2em) \( x \, y \) mapsto \( - 1 \)^r deg \( x y thin h^(g - 2 r) \) $

 is positive definite after extension of scalars to $bb(R)$. Thus the Hodge standard conjecture for $ell$-adic étale cohomology holds for abelian varieties in arbitrary characteristic.]
] <cc:standard>

#proof[
  Apply Milne’s Theorem 3.3 #cite(label("Milne2002Polarizations")) using Theorem 1. In Milne’s proof, the same Hodge hypothesis supplies the compatibility and positivity of the relevant polarizations through Theorem 2.1, read with the author’s addendum #cite(label("cc:Milne2002Addendum")) and the corrected auxiliary-field choice from #cite(label("cc:Milne1999Addenda")). Theorem 3.3 gives both the equality of equivalence relations and positivity on the primitive space of cycles modulo homological equivalence. The former identifies that primitive space with the numerical one above.
  The case $g = 0$ is immediate. For $g > 0$, choose a positive integer $m$ so that $m h$ is the class of a smooth hyperplane section; clearing denominators, taking a sufficiently large very ample multiple, and Bertini’s theorem permit this. Multiplication by the nonzero rational scalar $m^(g - 2 r + 1)$ does not change the primitive kernel, and the form for $m h$ is $m^(g - 2 r)$ times the form for $h$. This positive factor preserves positive definiteness. ◻
]

=== 8.4 Specialization of Hodge classes <specialization-of-hodge-classes>

#prodef([Remark 8.5.])[
  (Algebraicity of specialized Hodge classes).  Theorem 1 also combines with the CM transport of #cite(label("OpenAIMilneRationality2026")) Corollary 3.4 to give a specialization statement for abelian varieties that need not be CM. Fix a prime $p$ and a $p$-adic place of $overline(bb(Q))$. If $A \/ overline(bb(Q))$ has good reduction $A_0 \/ overline(bb(F))_p$ at that place, every rational Hodge class on $A$ specializes to the class of one rational algebraic cycle on $A_0$, simultaneously in all prime-to-$p$ realizations and in crystalline cohomology after extension to the completed field at the chosen place. Indeed, the transport writes that specialization as the pullback of a specialized CM Hodge class by a rational homomorphism of special fibers. Theorem 1 algebraizes the CM class; specialization and Chow pullback after clearing the homomorphism’s denominator give the required cycle. See #cite(label("OpenAIMilneRationality2026")) Corollary 6.4 for the field-of-definition argument and precise realization conventions, including $p = 2$. This application uses the CM transport, not the independent rationality proof of that paper, and is not an input to Theorem 1. It asserts algebraicity on the special fiber, not the Hodge conjecture for arbitrary complex abelian varieties.
] <cc:algebraic-specialization>


